B.push(p([t('Sections 3 to 7 establish structural facts about the lattice. None of them involves a measurement. It is reasonable to ask whether the coordinates carry any information about physical quantities, and this section reports a direct test.')]));
B.push(p([t('The quantity chosen is the enthalpy of hydride formation, ΔH_f, in kJ per mole of hydrogen. It is measured, tabulated, dimensional, and entirely independent of this framework. It is also the quantity that governs whether a metal absorbs hydrogen at all and how strongly it binds it. Values were taken for 39 transition metals, lanthanides and actinides for which binary hydride data are well established.')]));
B.push(p([t('Ordinary least squares on the lattice coordinates gives')]));
B.push(eq('ΔH_f  =  +136.2  −  89.5·ℓ  +  6.4·k        (kJ · mol⁻¹ H)', '7'));
B.push(p([t('with both coefficients significant — ℓ at p = 3.1 × 10⁻⁸, k at p = 8.1 × 10⁻⁴ — and an in-sample R² of 0.592. Because a two-parameter fit on 39 points can flatter itself, the model was validated leave-one-out, giving a cross-validated R² of 0.514 and an out-of-sample RMSE of 39 kJ · mol⁻¹ H. Results are shown in Figure 5.')]));
B.push(img('fig5b_hydride.png', 640, 243));
B.push(cap(5, 'The lattice tested against hydride formation enthalpy for 39 metals. (a) Measured against predicted values from the two-term model of Equation (7); the dashed line is parity. (b) Variance explained by candidate models, showing in-sample and leave-one-out cross-validated R². Atomic number Z alone has negative cross-validated R² — it carries no predictive information on this data — while the block index ℓ and the occupancy k together account for roughly half the variance. Adding |Q₀| or H_cp does not improve on ℓ + k, and the five-term model gains in-sample fit while losing it under cross-validation, the signature of overfitting. (c) Permutation null for the contribution of k: the observed gain of 0.15 in R² lies far outside the distribution obtained from 10,000 random shuffles of k (p = 0.0004).'));
B.push(p([t('Three points deserve emphasis, and the third is a caution.')]));
B.push(p([t('First, the ℓ term is not a discovery. That f-block metals form more stable hydrides than late d-block metals is long-established descriptive chemistry. The lattice recovers it because ℓ is the block index; it did not reveal it.')]));
B.push(p([t('Second, the k term is a genuine addition. Occupancy contributes 0.15 in R² beyond the block label, and the permutation test places this well outside chance. The direction is chemically sensible: within a block, hydride stability falls as the valence subshell fills, so early transition metals form stable hydrides and late ones do not. What the lattice supplies is that this trend is captured by the same coordinate that positions the element in configuration space, without additional parameters.')]));
B.push(p([t('Third — and this is why the section is placed here rather than in Part II — the classification by ΔQ direction is also informative. Class C elements average −61.9 kJ · mol⁻¹ H against −15.7 for Class B, a separation significant at p = 0.010 by Welch\'s test. The geometric sorting of Section 6, derived purely from the angular-momentum constraint, tracks a thermodynamic difference.')]));
B.push(p([t('The result establishes that the lattice has dimensional content. It does so empirically, by regression against measured values, and not by derivation: Equation (7) has coefficients in kJ · mol⁻¹ H because they were fitted to data in those units, not because the framework produced them. This is the same species of relationship as Pettifor\'s Mendeleev number or the Miedema scheme — a structure–property correlation, useful for screening, making no claim to first-principles status.')]));
B.push(p([t('It is equally important to state what the result does not establish. Hydride formation enthalpies are of order 10⁻¹ eV and are governed by electronic bonding. Coulomb barrier penetration involves energies of order 10⁵ eV and separations five orders of magnitude smaller. Demonstrating that lattice coordinates track bonding energetics carries no implication whatever for nuclear-scale quantities, and Section 15 returns to this point.')]));

B.push(h2('9.  Reserved Cells and Undiscovered Elements'));

