B.push(p([t('Section 10 established by exhaustive checking that Λ is closed under join and meet. That leaves the more useful question of why, and whether the closure is a coincidence of the particular numbers involved. It is not, and the reason places the lattice within a standard piece of order theory rather than establishing anything new. Stating it correctly also settles the fate of an extension proposed in an earlier draft of this work.')]));

B.push(h3('12.1  Λ as an intersection of sublattices'));
B.push(p([t('A subset of a product of chains that is closed under componentwise join and meet is a sublattice of that product, and finite intersections of sublattices are sublattices. The relevant observation is that certain elementary constraint types cut out sublattices of the full box, and that Λ is an intersection of exactly such sets.')]));
B.push(p([t('Constraints of the forms x_i ≤ x_j, x_i ≤ c and x_i ≥ c each define sublattices, as do constraints x_i ≤ f(x_j) with f monotone non-decreasing. What these share is that every coordinate appears with the same orientation on both sides: raising the operands raises both the constrained quantity and its bound, and lowering them lowers both. Direct verification on a 6 × 6 × 6 box confirms closure for each such constraint individually and for every pairwise and triple intersection tested.')]));
B.push(p([t('Λ is built this way. Starting from the box n ≤ 7, ℓ ≤ 4, k ≤ 18, which contains 630 cells and is trivially a sublattice, the constraint ℓ ≤ n − 1 reduces it to 450 cells and the constraint k ≤ 2(2ℓ+1) to the 210 admissible cells of Λ. Each step intersects with a sublattice, so closure is inherited rather than coincidental. The lower bounds n ≥ 1, ℓ ≥ 0 and k ≥ 1 are constants and impose no further condition.')]));
B.push(p([t('This is a standard argument and no novelty is claimed for it. What is worth recording is that the coordinates of Part I happen to satisfy the condition, which is why the results of Sections 10 and 11 hold.')]));

B.push(h3('12.2  Where the hydrogenic labelling fails'));
B.push(p([t('An earlier draft observed that the occupancy index k conflates the magnetic quantum number m with the spin projection, and proposed unfolding k into those constituents in the hope of admitting the SO(4,2) representation theory that the three-axis form cannot support. The account just given shows why that cannot work, and locates the obstruction precisely.')]));
B.push(p([t('The hydrogenic constraint is |m| ≤ ℓ, which is the conjunction of two inequalities: m ≤ ℓ and −m ≤ ℓ. The first is of the permitted form. The second is not, because the coordinate m enters negated, and orientation is reversed: raising m makes the left-hand side smaller rather than larger. Constraints in which a coordinate appears with mixed orientation do not in general define sublattices, and the same defect appears in familiar examples such as |x − 2| ≤ y, which fails closure on a 6 × 6 box.')]));
