B.push(h2('13.  The Periodic System as an Order Ideal'));
B.push(p([t('Sections 10 to 12 concern the lattice as a whole: its closure, its slices, and why the coordinates behave as they do. This section concerns the position within that lattice of the 118 cells that are actually occupied, and reports a property of that subset which does not appear to have been noted.')]));

B.push(h3('13.1  The occupied cells are downward closed'));
B.push(p([t('An order ideal, or down-set, of a poset is a subset closed downward: if x belongs to it, so does every element below x. Testing this for the occupied cells of Λ — for every occupied x and every admissible y with y ≤ x componentwise, is y also occupied? — returns no violations across all 118 cells. The downward closure of the occupied set is exactly the occupied set.')]));
B.push(p([t('In chemical terms the statement is this. If an element exists whose ground-state valence configuration is (n, ℓ, k), then every configuration with no larger shell, no larger subshell and no larger occupancy is likewise realised by some element. There are no gaps beneath any occupied cell.')]));
B.push(img('fig9_ideal.png', 640, 211));
B.push(cap(9, 'The periodic system as an order ideal of Λ. (a) The 210 admissible cells arranged by shell, with the 118 occupied cells filled. The occupied region is closed downward; the three maximal cells are outlined in green and the five cells covering them are outlined in gold. (b) For each occupied cell x, the number of admissible cells below it against the number of those that are occupied. All 118 points lie on the diagonal, with counts ranging from 1 to 92: the down-set property holds without exception. (c) The discovery frontier. Any element beyond those known must occupy a cell covering one of the three maximal cells, giving five candidate addresses in configuration space.'));

B.push(h3('13.2  Why it holds'));
B.push(p([t('The property is not a coincidence, and the reason is worth stating because it distinguishes this result from numerical accidents of the kind discussed in Section 13.4. Elements are populated by aufbau filling: electrons are added one at a time, and the sequence of ground-state configurations traces a monotone path through the lattice. A set generated by a monotone accumulation of this kind is closed downward by construction. The order ideal property is the order-theoretic shadow of the filling process.')]));
B.push(p([t('This also explains why the property survives the known irregularities. The lanthanide and actinide anomalies, which place 28 inversions in the Madelung ordering of Section 11, alter which cell is reached at which atomic number but do not remove any cell from the accumulated set. Ordering is disturbed; downward closure is not.')]));

B.push(h3('13.3  Consequences'));
B.push(p([t('Three consequences follow, of decreasing strength.')]));
B.push(p([t('The first is structural. Λ is distributive, since componentwise minimum and maximum on integer tuples satisfy the distributive laws; direct checking confirms this and identifies 27 join-irreducible cells from which all 210 are generated. By Birkhoff\'s representation theorem the order ideals of a finite poset themselves form a distributive lattice, so the periodic system corresponds to a single element of the lattice J(Λ) of ideals of Λ. The historical growth of the known elements is then a monotone path upward through J(Λ), each discovery moving the system to a cell covering its predecessor. This is a description rather than a prediction, but it is an exact one.')]));
B.push(p([t('The second concerns the frontier. The maximal occupied cells — those with no occupied cell above them — number exactly three: (5, 3, 14) held by lawrencium, (6, 2, 10) held by copernicium, and (7, 1, 6) held by oganesson. Because the occupied set is an ideal, it can grow only by acquiring a cell that covers one of these. There are five such cells: (5, 4, 14), (6, 3, 10), (6, 3, 14), (7, 2, 6) and (7, 2, 10). Any element beyond those presently known must occupy one of these five addresses in configuration space, or else extend the lattice beyond n = 7.')]));
B.push(p([t('The limits of that second consequence require emphasis, and they are the same limits Section 9 places on the reserved cells. The claim constrains valence configuration only. It says nothing about nuclear stability, synthesis cross-sections, half-lives, or whether a nucleus placed at such an address would persist long enough to be observed, all of which are governed by nuclear structure rather than by electron bookkeeping. The statement is that if a new element is characterised, its valence configuration will be found at one of five coordinates — not that any of the five will be reached.')]));
B.push(p([t('The third is a caution about interpretation. The lattice is a lossless recoding of the element list rather than a compression of it. The joint entropy of the coordinates across the 118 occupied cells is 6.883 bits, exactly log₂(118), matching the entropy of a flat enumeration; the assignment is injective and no information is discarded or created. What the coordinates buy is organisation — the structures of Sections 10 to 13 — and not a reduction in the descriptive burden.')]));

B.push(h3('13.4  A coincidence that is only a coincidence'));
B.push(p([t('One numerical observation invites a correspondence that does not hold, and it is recorded here so that others do not pursue it. The lattice contains exactly 210 admissible cells, and 210 is the binomial coefficient C(10, 4), which counts the partitions fitting inside a 4 × 6 box and hence the elements of a Young lattice of that shape. The coincidence is suggestive.')]));
B.push(p([t('It does not survive examination. Two graded posets of equal cardinality are isomorphic only if their rank generating functions agree, and these do not. Ranking Λ by n + ℓ + k gives level sizes 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 13, 14, 15, 15, 14, whereas the corresponding Gaussian binomial coefficient gives 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 13, 14, 16, 16. The two structures differ from the second rank onward. Λ is not a Young lattice, and the equality of cardinalities carries no structural content.')]));

