B.push(h3('10.3  Two further invariants'));
B.push(p([t('Two properties of Λ are worth recording, both established by direct computation and both bearing on how the lattice compares with familiar combinatorial structures.')]));
B.push(p([t('The first is the Sperner property. A graded poset has this property when no antichain exceeds the largest rank level in size. Products of chains possess it automatically, being rank-symmetric and unimodal, but Λ is not rank-symmetric, as Section 13.5 shows, so the property cannot be inherited and must be checked. Computing the maximum antichain by Dilworth\'s theorem, via a maximum matching in the comparability graph, returns 15 — exactly the size of the largest rank level, at n + ℓ + k = 11. Λ is Sperner.')]));
B.push(p([t('The second is a parity invariant. The rank generating function of Λ decomposes as a sum of shifted q-integers, one per column,')]));
B.push(eq('F(q) = Σ(n,ℓ)  q^(n+ℓ+1) · [ 2(2ℓ+1) ]_q ,     [m]_q = 1 + q + … + q^(m−1)', '9'));
B.push(p([t('an identity verified symbolically. Every column capacity 2(2ℓ+1) is even, and [m]_q vanishes at q = −1 whenever m is even, so F(−1) = 0 identically. The lattice therefore contains exactly as many cells of even rank as of odd: 105 of each. The occupied set inherits the balance exactly, at 59 and 59, because it fills whole columns as Section 13.2 establishes.')]));
B.push(p([t('The factor of two making every capacity even is electron spin. The parity balance is thus a structural consequence of spin degeneracy rather than a numerical accident of the particular ceilings chosen, and it would persist under any extension of the shell or subshell limits.')]));

