cd /home/claude/paper && cp ../fig07_dimension.png . && cat > /tmp/s101.js << 'SEC'
B.push(h3('10.1  The order dimension of Λ is three'));
B.push(p([t('Part I assigned three coordinates to each element without arguing that three were necessary. Two would be simpler and the conventional table manages with two axes; the question is whether the third carries information that cannot be recovered otherwise. Order dimension answers it precisely.')]));
B.push(p([t('The order dimension of a poset, introduced by Dushnik and Miller, is the least number of linear orders whose intersection reproduces the partial order. A poset of dimension two can be encoded by two rankings: x precedes y in the partial order exactly when it precedes y in both. Dimension three means no such pair of rankings exists.')]));
B.push(p([t('Since Λ is a subset of ℕ³ ordered componentwise, and the three coordinate projections are linear orders whose intersection is precisely the componentwise order, the dimension is at most three. To establish that it is exactly three it suffices to exhibit a standard example S₃: three elements a₁, a₂, a₃ and three elements b₁, b₂, b₃ with aᵢ ≤ bⱼ if and only if i ≠ j. Such a configuration cannot be realised by two linear orders, so its presence forces dimension at least three.')]));
B.push(p([t('The cells')]));
B.push(eq('a₁ = (7,2,6)   a₂ = (5,4,6)   a₃ = (5,2,8)', ''));
B.push(eq('b₁ = (5,4,8)   b₂ = (7,2,8)   b₃ = (7,4,6)', '9'));
B.push(p([t('are all admissible, and each aᵢ lies below exactly those bⱼ with j ≠ i, as Figure 7 shows. Each pair differs from the base cell (5, 2, 6) by an increment of two in one coordinate for the minimals and in two coordinates for the maximals, which is what produces the cyclic incidence pattern. Hence dim(Λ) = 3 exactly.')]));
B.push(img('fig07_dimension.png', 640, 266));
B.push(cap(7, 'Order dimension of the lattice. (a) Six admissible cells forming a standard example S₃: each minimal aᵢ lies below exactly two of the three maximals, omitting bᵢ. (b) The incidence pattern, comparable off the diagonal and incomparable on it. A pair of linear orders cannot reproduce it. (c) The witness located within Λ, other admissible cells shown faintly. Since Λ ⊆ ℕ³ gives dim ≤ 3 and the witness gives dim ≥ 3, the order dimension is exactly three.'));
B.push(p([t('The consequence is worth stating carefully, because the result is easy to overread. What it establishes is that no two linear orders can encode the containment relation on Λ: any two-coordinate scheme, however contrived, must lose order information. In that specific sense the three coordinates are necessary rather than merely convenient, and the periodic system is irreducibly three-dimensional as an ordered set.')]));
B.push(p([t('What it does not establish is anything about physical space. Order dimension is a property of a relation, not a spatial claim, and the axes remain counts of nodes and electrons as Section 19 emphasises. Nor does it imply that two-dimensional periodic tables are deficient for their purposes: a layout may usefully display chemical similarity without encoding the componentwise order at all, and the conventional table does exactly that. The claim is confined to the order relation defined in Section 2.')]));
B.push(p([t('The witness given here occupies cells at ℓ = 4, the g-subshell, which no known element reaches. This is legitimate for a statement about Λ, whose admissible region includes those cells by construction, but it means the result concerns the lattice rather than the occupied set alone. Restricting attention to the 118 occupied cells would require a separate argument, which is not attempted here.')]));
B.push(p([t('The technique is standard — the standard example and the Dushnik–Miller characterisation are textbook material, and the argument above is an application rather than a development. No search of the order-theoretic literature has been undertaken beyond the works discussed in Section 14, so novelty is not claimed; the result is offered because it justifies the construction of Part I, not because it is believed to be new.')]));

