// ═══════════════════ PART II ═══════════════════
B.push(new Paragraph({children:[new PageBreak()]}));
B.push(h1('PART II — THE LATTICE AS AN ORDERED SET'));
B.push(p([t('Prefatory note. ',{bold:true}),t('This part treats Λ as an abstract ordered set, without reference to which of its cells are occupied. The results are structural facts about the coordinate system rather than correlations with data; each can be checked by direct computation, and the computations are exhaustive rather than sampled wherever the search space permits. Several of them explain why the construction of Part I works at all. The techniques used — the Dushnik–Miller characterisation of order dimension, Dilworth\'s theorem, Birkhoff representation, the sublattice argument — are standard, and no novelty is claimed for them; what is offered is their application to the configuration lattice.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:200}}));

B.push(h2('7.  The Order Dimension of Λ is Three'));
B.push(p([t('Part I assigned three coordinates without arguing that three were necessary. Two would be simpler, and the conventional table manages with two axes; the question is whether the third carries information that cannot be recovered otherwise. Order dimension answers it precisely.')]));
B.push(p([t('The order dimension of a poset, introduced by Dushnik and Miller in 1941, is the least number of linear orders whose intersection reproduces the partial order. A poset of dimension two can be encoded by two rankings: x precedes y in the partial order exactly when it precedes y in both. Dimension three means no such pair of rankings exists, however cleverly constructed.')]));
B.push(p([t('Since Λ is a subset of ℤ³ ordered componentwise, and the three coordinate projections are linear orders whose intersection is precisely the componentwise order, the dimension is at most three. To establish that it is exactly three it suffices to exhibit a standard example S₃: three elements a₁, a₂, a₃ and three elements b₁, b₂, b₃ with aᵢ ≤ bⱼ if and only if i ≠ j. Such a configuration cannot be realised by two linear orders, because in any linear order the three aᵢ are totally ordered and the greatest of them must precede every bⱼ that any of them precedes — which the incidence pattern forbids. Its presence therefore forces dimension at least three.')]));
B.push(p([t('The cells')]));
B.push(eq('a₁ = (7,2,6)     a₂ = (5,4,6)     a₃ = (5,2,8)',''));
B.push(eq('b₁ = (5,4,8)     b₂ = (7,2,8)     b₃ = (7,4,6)','7'));
B.push(p([t('are all admissible under Equation (1), and each aᵢ lies below exactly those bⱼ with j ≠ i, as Figure 4 shows. The construction is systematic rather than found by search: taking a base cell (5, 2, 6), the minimals are obtained by adding two to a single coordinate and the maximals by adding two to each of the other pair, which produces the cyclic incidence automatically. Hence dim(Λ) = 3 exactly.')]));
B.push(img('F03_dimension.png',640,266));
B.push(cap(4,'Order dimension of the lattice. (a) Six admissible cells forming a standard example S₃: each minimal aᵢ lies below exactly two of the three maximals, omitting bᵢ. (b) The incidence pattern, comparable off the diagonal and incomparable on it; a pair of linear orders cannot reproduce it. (c) The witness located within Λ, other admissible cells shown faintly. Since Λ ⊆ ℤ³ gives dim ≤ 3 and the witness gives dim ≥ 3, the order dimension is exactly three.'));
B.push(p([t('The consequence deserves careful statement, because the result is easy to overread. What it establishes is that no two linear orders can encode the containment relation on Λ: any two-coordinate scheme, however contrived, must lose order information. In that specific sense the three coordinates are necessary rather than merely convenient, and the periodic system is irreducibly three-dimensional as an ordered set.')]));
B.push(p([t('What it does not establish is anything about physical space. Order dimension is a property of a relation, not a spatial claim, and the axes remain counts of nodes and electrons as Part V emphasises. Nor does it imply that two-dimensional periodic tables are deficient for their purposes: a layout may usefully display chemical similarity without encoding the componentwise order at all, and the conventional table does exactly that. The claim is confined to the order relation defined in Equation (1). Note also that the witness given here occupies cells at ℓ = 4, the g-subshell, which no known element reaches; the result concerns the admissible lattice rather than the occupied set, and restricting to the 118 occupied cells would require a separate argument not attempted here.')]));

B.push(h2('8.  Λ is a Bounded Distributive Lattice'));
B.push(p([t('Order the admissible cells componentwise: (n, ℓ, k) ≤ (n′, ℓ′, k′) when each coordinate is no greater than its counterpart. The join of two cells is their componentwise maximum and the meet their componentwise minimum. The question is whether Λ is closed under both operations — whether these are again admissible.')]));
B.push(p([t('Exhaustive checking of all 21,945 pairs drawn from the 210 admissible cells returns no failures in either direction. Λ is therefore a lattice in the algebraic sense, and it is bounded, with least element (1, 0, 1) and greatest element (7, 4, 18). It is moreover distributive, since componentwise minimum and maximum on integer tuples satisfy the distributive laws identically; direct checking confirms this and identifies twenty-seven join-irreducible cells from which all 210 are generated. By Birkhoff\'s representation theorem the order ideals of a finite poset themselves form a distributive lattice, a fact Part III uses.')]));
B.push(p([t('One consequence should be recorded immediately. Hydrogen was placed at the origin in Section 2 as a reference choice. It is not a choice: (1, 0, 1) is the unique minimum of the poset, the only cell below every other, and its position is a theorem about the structure rather than a convention imposed on it. A related point settles a question that might otherwise seem open. The quantities ΔQ and σ²(t) are differences of coordinates and are invariant under any translation of the origin; so are the direction classes of Section 6 and the adjacency of Section 17. Order-theoretic facts are likewise preserved, since a coordinate shift is an order isomorphism. Nothing that works in this framework depends on where the origin is placed.')]));

B.push(h2('9.  Why These Coordinates and Not Others'));
B.push(p([t('Section 8 established closure by exhaustion. That leaves the more useful question of why, and whether the closure is an accident of the particular numbers involved. It is not, and the reason places the lattice within a standard piece of order theory.')]));
B.push(h3('9.1  Λ as an intersection of sublattices'));
B.push(p([t('A subset of a product of chains that is closed under componentwise join and meet is a sublattice of that product, and finite intersections of sublattices are sublattices (Birkhoff 1967). The relevant observation is that certain elementary constraint types cut out sublattices of the full box, and that Λ is an intersection of exactly such sets.')]));
B.push(p([t('Constraints of the forms xᵢ ≤ xⱼ, xᵢ ≤ c and xᵢ ≥ c each define sublattices, as do constraints xᵢ ≤ f(xⱼ) with f monotone non-decreasing. What these share is that every coordinate appears with the same orientation on both sides: raising the operands raises both the constrained quantity and its bound, and lowering them lowers both. Writing the criterion out,')]));
B.push(eq('ℓ ≤ n − 1            upper bound, monotone in n','8'));
B.push(eq('k ≤ 2(2ℓ + 1)        upper bound, monotone in ℓ','9'));
B.push(eq('n ≥ 1,  ℓ ≥ 0,  k ≥ 1        constant floors','10'));
B.push(p([t('every constraint defining Λ is of a permitted form. Building the lattice constraint by constraint confirms it: the box n ≤ 7, ℓ ≤ 4, k ≤ 18 contains 630 cells and is trivially a sublattice; imposing ℓ ≤ n − 1 reduces it to 450 cells and preserves closure; imposing k ≤ 2(2ℓ+1) gives the 210 admissible cells of Λ and preserves it again. Closure is inherited rather than coincidental. No novelty is claimed for the argument; what is worth recording is that the coordinates of Part I happen to satisfy the condition, which is why the results of this part hold at all.')]));
B.push(h3('9.2  Where the hydrogenic labelling fails'));
B.push(p([t('An earlier draft observed that the occupancy index k conflates the magnetic quantum number m with the spin projection, and proposed unfolding k into those constituents in the hope of admitting the SO(4,2) representation theory that the three-axis form cannot support. The criterion just stated shows why that cannot work, and locates the obstruction precisely.')]));
B.push(p([t('The hydrogenic constraint is |m| ≤ ℓ, which is the conjunction of m ≤ ℓ and −m ≤ ℓ. The first is of the permitted form. The second is not, because the coordinate m enters negated and orientation is reversed: raising m makes the left-hand side smaller rather than larger. Constraints in which a coordinate appears with mixed orientation do not in general define sublattices, and the same defect appears in familiar examples such as |x − 2| ≤ y, which fails closure on a 6 × 6 box.')]));
B.push(p([t('Building Λ′ = {(n, ℓ, m, s)} constraint by constraint shows the failure entering at a single identifiable step. The hydrogenic box is a sublattice; adding ℓ ≤ n − 1 leaves 450 cells and preserves closure; adding m ≤ ℓ leaves 330 cells and still preserves it; adding −m ≤ ℓ gives the 210 admissible cells and destroys it. Exhaustive checking of the resulting set returns zero join failures and 4,040 meet failures. A concrete instance makes the mechanism plain: the cells (ℓ, m) = (3, −3) and (1, 1) are both admissible, their componentwise meet is (1, −3), and |−3| > 1.')]));
B.push(img('F04_closure.png',640,211));
B.push(cap(5,'Why the occupancy coordinate is load-bearing. (a) The constraint on k: a ceiling rising with ℓ and a constant floor at k = 1, both monotone in the same orientation, so meets remain admissible. (b) The hydrogenic constraint on m: the ceiling m ≤ ℓ is permitted, but the floor m ≥ −ℓ places m on the left with reversed orientation, and the meet of (3, −3) with (1, 1) falls outside. (c) Closure across four coordinate systems, each containing exactly 210 cells. Only the signed hydrogenic labelling fails, and it fails on meet alone.'));
B.push(p([t('Closure can be restored by substituting m′ = m + ℓ, which converts the pair into m′ ≤ 2ℓ and m′ ≥ 0 — a monotone ceiling and a constant floor, both permitted — and verification confirms that the shifted four-axis set is again closed. But the repair defeats its own purpose. The coordinate m′ is not the magnetic quantum number; it is m displaced by an amount that varies across the lattice. The representation theory that motivated the unfolding acts on m, not on m shifted by a position-dependent offset, and so has no more purchase on the shifted lattice than on the original. The proposal is therefore withdrawn: no four-axis refactoring of Λ both preserves the order structure and exposes the hydrogenic labels in a form on which group theory can act.')]));
B.push(p([t('The positive content of this section is that the occupancy coordinate k, introduced in Part I as nothing more than an electron count, is the component that makes the structure work. Its bounds carry no orientation reversal, and the results of this part would not survive its replacement by the hydrogenic labels it conflates. Section 24 finds the same coordinate carrying the only chemical information in the lattice that established descriptors do not already encode.')]));

B.push(h2('10.  Invariants'));
B.push(h3('10.1  Shell capacity as lattice volume'));
B.push(p([t('Counting the admissible cells at fixed n gives')]));
B.push(eq('Σ (ℓ = 0 to min(n−1,4))  2(2ℓ+1)  =  2n²        for n ≤ 5','11'));
B.push(p([t('so the shell capacities 2, 8, 18, 32 and 50 are the volumes of the horizontal slices of Λ. The Pauli capacities of the periodic system are not an input to the lattice; they are what one obtains by counting it.')]));
B.push(p([t('The identity is stated with its restriction because the restriction is real. For n = 6 and n = 7 the ceiling ℓ ≤ 4 truncates the sum, which returns 50 in both cases rather than the 72 and 98 that 2n² would require. Extending the lattice to ℓ ≤ n−1 for all n would restore the identity in general; the truncation is a consequence of the modelling choice made in Equation (1) and not of the geometry. An earlier draft of this work stated the identity without qualification, and that statement was incorrect.')]));
B.push(h3('10.2  The Sperner property'));
B.push(p([t('A graded poset has the Sperner property when no antichain exceeds the largest rank level in size. Products of chains possess it automatically, being rank-symmetric and unimodal, but Λ is not rank-symmetric, as Section 10.4 shows, so the property cannot be inherited and must be checked. Computing the maximum antichain by Dilworth\'s theorem, via a maximum matching in the comparability graph, returns 15 — exactly the size of the largest rank level, which occurs at n + ℓ + k = 11. Λ is Sperner.')]));
B.push(h3('10.3  A parity invariant forced by spin'));
B.push(p([t('The rank generating function of Λ, grading by n + ℓ + k, decomposes as a sum of shifted q-integers, one per (n, ℓ) column:')]));
B.push(eq('F(q)  =  Σ(n,ℓ)  q^(n+ℓ+1) · [ 2(2ℓ+1) ]_q ,      [m]_q = 1 + q + … + q^(m−1)','12'));
B.push(p([t('an identity verified symbolically. Every column capacity 2(2ℓ+1) is even, and [m]_q vanishes at q = −1 whenever m is even, so F(−1) = 0 identically. The lattice therefore contains exactly as many cells of even rank as of odd: 105 of each. The occupied set inherits the balance exactly, at 59 and 59, because it fills whole columns as Section 19 establishes. The factor of two making every capacity even is electron spin, so the parity balance is a structural consequence of spin degeneracy rather than a numerical accident of the particular ceilings chosen, and it would persist under any extension of the shell or subshell limits.')]));
B.push(h3('10.4  The rank sequence is not symmetric'));
B.push(p([t('The full rank sequence of Λ is 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 13, 14, 15, 15, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10, 9, 8, 7, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 3, 3, 2, 1 — unimodal, with its maximum at rank 11, but not palindromic. Gaussian binomials are rank-symmetric, as are Boolean lattices, divisor lattices and the Young lattices of a box, so a lattice whose rank sequence fails to be symmetric cannot be isomorphic to any of them. The asymmetry arises from the interaction of the two ceilings: ℓ ≤ n−1 truncates the higher subshells at small n while k ≤ 2(2ℓ+1) permits the tall f and g columns only where ℓ is already large, so the lattice is broader near its base than near its summit.')]));
B.push(p([t('This settles a numerical coincidence that would otherwise invite pursuit. Λ contains exactly 210 admissible cells, and 210 is the binomial coefficient C(10, 4), which counts the partitions fitting inside a 4 × 6 box and hence the elements of a Young lattice of that shape. Two graded posets of equal cardinality are isomorphic only if their rank generating functions agree, and these do not: the Gaussian binomial gives 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 13, 14, 16, 16, differing from Λ from the second rank onward. Λ is not a Young lattice, and the equality of cardinalities carries no structural content. It is recorded here so that others need not pursue it.')]));

