B.push(p([t('The full rank sequence of Λ is 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 13, 14, 15, 15, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10, 9, 8, 7, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 3, 3, 2, 1 — unimodal, with its maximum at rank 11, but not palindromic. Gaussian binomials are rank-symmetric, as are Boolean lattices, divisor lattices and the Young lattices of a box, so a lattice whose rank sequence fails to be symmetric cannot be isomorphic to any of them. The asymmetry arises from the interaction of the two ceilings: ℓ ≤ n−1 truncates the higher subshells at small n while k ≤ 2(2ℓ+1) permits the tall f and g columns only where ℓ is already large, so the lattice is broader near its base than near its summit.')]));
B.push(p([t('This settles a numerical coincidence that would otherwise invite pursuit. Λ contains exactly 210 admissible cells, and 210 is the binomial coefficient C(10, 4), which counts the partitions fitting inside a 4 × 6 box and hence the elements of a Young lattice of that shape. Two graded posets of equal cardinality are isomorphic only if their rank generating functions agree, and these do not: the Gaussian binomial gives 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 13, 14, 16, 16, differing from Λ from the second rank onward. Λ is not a Young lattice, and the equality of cardinalities carries no structural content. It is recorded here so that others need not pursue it.')]));

