B.push(h2('11.  Madelung Ordering and the Janet Correspondence'));
B.push(h3('11.1  The Madelung rule as a linear functional'));
B.push(p([t('The Madelung or aufbau rule fills subshells in order of increasing n + ℓ, ties broken by increasing n. On the lattice, n + ℓ is a linear functional, and its level sets are parallel diagonal planes cutting through Λ. The filling sequence of the elements is a sweep of these planes.')]));
B.push(p([t('Sorting the 118 occupied cells by (n + ℓ, n, k) and reading off atomic number reproduces the true ordering with Kendall τ = 0.90 on the raw functional; the departures are the known lanthanide and actinide irregularities, where measured ground states diverge from the idealised rule. The lattice is compatible with Madelung ordering but does not derive it: the rule remains an empirical input, imposed on Λ rather than produced by it. It is worth recording that Λ\'s own rank functional n + ℓ + k is a considerably poorer proxy for atomic number, at τ = 0.50 with 1,558 discordant pairs among 6,903, so the Madelung functional is not merely one grading among many.')]));
B.push(p([t('What the lattice does supply is an explanation for the pattern of period lengths. Computing the volume of each Madelung slice gives 2, 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32, 32, 50, 50, 72, 72 — the slices come in exactly equal consecutive pairs. This pairing is the reason the periods of the periodic table repeat their lengths, a regularity the conventional two-dimensional layout must impose by construction and cannot explain. Here it is a counting fact about parallel slices of a three-dimensional structure.')]));
B.push(img('F06_madelung_janet.png',640,203));
B.push(cap(6,'Madelung structure of the lattice. (a) The (n, ℓ) face of Λ, each cell carrying its capacity 2(2ℓ+1) and shaded by Madelung level; the dashed diagonals are the planes n + ℓ = constant. (b) Volume of each Madelung slice. The slices pair exactly — 2, 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32, 32, 50, 50 — reproducing the observed period lengths. (c) The Janet left-step table, obtained by projecting Λ along the functional n + ℓ; each row is one Madelung slice and its width is the slice volume from panel (b).'));
B.push(h3('11.2  Correspondence with the Janet left-step table'));
B.push(p([t('The projection just described is not a new arrangement. Charles Janet proposed in 1929 a reordering of the periodic table by n + ℓ blocks, producing period lengths 2, 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32, 32 and placing helium above beryllium rather than above neon. The left-step table has been argued in the philosophy of chemistry to be the most natural periodic form, on the grounds that it follows the quantum-mechanical filling order rather than chemical resemblance.')]));
B.push(p([t('The relationship to the present framework is exact. Janet\'s blocks are the level sets of the linear functional n + ℓ on Λ, and his table is the two-dimensional projection of the lattice along that functional. This is a correspondence rather than an analogy: the two structures contain the same information, with Janet\'s form arising from Λ by a specific and identifiable projection. The claim this supports is modest but real. The left-step table is customarily justified by appeal to the Madelung rule; the lattice shows that it is the shadow of a three-dimensional object, and that the pairing of period lengths which Janet\'s form exhibits is a volumetric property of that object.')]));

B.push(h2('12.  Fibres, the Lattice Boundary, and Rigidity'));
B.push(h3('12.1  Chemical groups as fibres'));
B.push(p([t('Section 11 concerns the projection of Λ along n + ℓ. A second and independent relationship emerges from a different operation on the same lattice: fixing the pair (ℓ, k) and allowing n to vary.')]));
B.push(p([t('Each such fibre is a line through Λ parallel to the n-axis, and the elements lying on it are precisely a conventional chemical group. Fixing (ℓ, k) = (0, 1) gives hydrogen, lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, caesium and francium; (1, 4) gives oxygen, sulphur, selenium, tellurium, polonium and livermorium; and so across the main groups. The columns of the two-dimensional table are one-dimensional fibres of the three-dimensional lattice, and the periodicity that the conventional layout represents by adjacency of columns is here a statement about parallel lines.')]));
B.push(p([t('Seven of the eight main groups are reproduced exactly. The single departure is instructive. Helium has valence configuration 1s², placing it at (1, 0, 2) and hence on the fibre (ℓ, k) = (0, 2) together with beryllium, magnesium, calcium and the remaining alkaline earths — not on the fibre (1, 6) occupied by neon, argon and the other noble gases. That two independent operations on Λ — projection along a linear functional in Section 11.2, and fibration along the n-axis here — converge on the same contested placement is a point in favour of the arrangement, though the question of whether helium is better classified by electron configuration or by chemical behaviour is one on which the philosophy-of-chemistry literature remains divided, and nothing here settles it.')]));
B.push(h3('12.2  The lattice boundary and kainosymmetry'));
B.push(p([t('A further correspondence concerns the diagonal ℓ = n − 1, where the angular-momentum constraint is tight. The cells on this diagonal are 1s, 2p, 3d, 4f and 5g: the subshells that are first of their kind, possessing no radial node.')]));
B.push(p([t('These subshells are anomalous in well-documented ways. The phenomenon is known as kainosymmetry or primogenic repulsion: a first-of-kind subshell is unusually compact, is not screened by an inner subshell of the same ℓ, and produces the irregularities of the first short period, the anomalous properties of the 3d series relative to 4d and 5d, and part of the divergence of the lanthanides from the actinides. In the conventional table these anomalies appear as scattered exceptions distributed across the layout. In Λ they are the boundary — exactly the cells where ℓ attains its maximum permitted value for the given n. This is a structural observation rather than an explanation: the lattice does not say why a first-of-kind subshell should be compact, but it does locate a known family of chemical anomalies at a single identifiable feature of the geometry.')]));
B.push(img('F07_fibres.png',640,211));
B.push(cap(7,'Fibres, the lattice boundary, and parity. (a) Fixing (ℓ, k) and varying n traces a fibre of Λ; each fibre is a conventional chemical group. The fibre (ℓ, k) = (0, 2) contains helium alongside beryllium and the alkaline earths rather than the noble gases. (b) The kainosymmetric subshells 1s, 2p, 3d, 4f and 5g occupy the diagonal ℓ = n − 1, the boundary at which the constraint ℓ ≤ n − 1 is tight. (c) Parity balance: Λ divides evenly between cells of even and odd rank, and the occupied set inherits the division, a consequence of every column capacity being even.'));
B.push(h3('12.3  The orientation of the lattice is rigid'));
B.push(p([t('Section 8 showed that translations of the origin change nothing. A stronger question is whether the ordering of values along an axis is likewise free. It is not.')]));
B.push(p([t('Permuting which subshell type occupies each position on the ℓ-axis, carrying the Pauli capacity with the position, gives 120 candidate arrangements. Of these, exactly one both accommodates the 118 elements and leaves the occupied set downward closed: the familiar s < p < d < f < g. Every other arrangement either cannot admit the elements at all, because some element would require an occupancy exceeding the capacity available at its position, or admits them while destroying the order-ideal property of Section 18. The subshell ordering is forced.')]));
B.push(p([t('A similar uniqueness holds for the Madelung functional. Of the twenty-six functionals an + bℓ + ck with small non-negative integer coefficients, exactly two reproduce the period-length sequence 2, 2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32, 32: the Madelung functional (1, 1, 0) and its scalar multiple (2, 2, 0). Every other combination gives monotone growth or an unrelated sequence. The Madelung rule is not one choice among many that happen to work — it is essentially unique within this family.')]));
B.push(img('F05_rigidity.png',640,206));
B.push(cap(8,'Rigidity of the lattice orientation. (a) Of 120 orderings of the subshell types along the ℓ-axis, exactly one admits the 118 elements and preserves downward closure. (b) Of twenty-six shear functionals an + bℓ + ck with small integer coefficients, only the Madelung functional (1,1,0) and its multiple (2,2,0) reproduce the period lengths; marker size encodes c. (c) Occupied-column counts by shell, against the number admitted. Shells 5, 6 and 7 are short by one, two and three columns; the resulting equality of profiles between shells 2 and 7, 3 and 6, 4 and 5 generates eight permutations that leave the occupied set unchanged, but these are artifacts of truncation at Z = 118 and are not symmetries of Λ.'));
B.push(p([t('The n-axis behaves differently, and the difference is worth recording because it could be mistaken for a symmetry. Eight permutations of the shell labels leave the occupied set downward closed, and they form a group isomorphic to (ℤ/2)³ generated by the transpositions (2,7), (3,6) and (4,5). The reason is that these shell pairs have identical occupied (n, ℓ) profiles — shells 2 and 7 both hold s and p only, 3 and 6 hold s, p and d, 4 and 5 hold s, p, d and f — so the swaps are invisible to any test that examines only the occupied set. But the maps are not automorphisms of Λ: applying the (2,7) swap to the admissible lattice sends 42 cells outside it, because shell 7 admits ℓ up to 4 while shell 2 admits only ℓ ≤ 1. The apparent symmetry is a property of where element production stopped, not of the geometry, and it is reported here as a negative result.')]));

// ═══════════════════ PART III ═══════════════════
B.push(new Paragraph({children:[new PageBreak()]}));
B.push(h1('PART III — THE PERIODIC SYSTEM WITHIN THE LATTICE'));
B.push(p([t('Prefatory note. ',{bold:true}),t('Part II established what kind of object Λ is. This part asks a different question: of the 210 admissible cells, 118 are occupied by known elements — where does that subset sit within the structure, and does its position have a character of its own? The answer is that it does, and that the property in question follows from the physics of how elements are populated rather than from the geometry of the lattice.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:200}}));

B.push(h2('13.  The Occupied Cells Form an Order Ideal'));
B.push(p([t('An order ideal, or down-set, of a poset is a subset closed downward: if x belongs to it, so does every element below x. Testing this for the occupied cells of Λ — for every occupied x and every admissible y with y ≤ x componentwise, is y also occupied? — returns no violations across all 118 cells. The downward closure of the occupied set is exactly the occupied set.')]));
B.push(p([t('In chemical terms the statement is this. If an element exists whose ground-state valence configuration is (n, ℓ, k), then every configuration with no larger shell, no larger subshell and no larger occupancy is likewise realised by some element. There are no gaps beneath any occupied cell. The test is not trivially satisfied: the number of admissible cells lying below an occupied cell ranges from one to ninety-two across the 118, and every one of those cells is occupied.')]));
B.push(img('F10_ideal.png',640,211));
B.push(cap(9,'The periodic system as an order ideal of Λ. (a) The 210 admissible cells arranged by shell, with the 118 occupied cells filled. The occupied region is closed downward; the three maximal cells are outlined in green and the five cells covering them in gold. (b) For each occupied cell x, the number of admissible cells below it against the number of those that are occupied. All 118 points lie on the diagonal, with counts ranging from 1 to 92. (c) The discovery frontier: any element beyond those known must occupy a cell covering one of the three maximal cells, giving five candidate addresses.'));
B.push(p([t('The property is not a coincidence, and the reason is worth stating because it distinguishes this result from the numerical accident discussed in Section 10.4. Elements are populated by aufbau filling: electrons are added one at a time, and the sequence of ground-state configurations traces a monotone path through the lattice. A set generated by a monotone accumulation of this kind is closed downward by construction. The order-ideal property is the order-theoretic shadow of the filling process. This also explains why the property survives the known irregularities: the lanthanide and actinide anomalies alter which cell is reached at which atomic number but do not remove any cell from the accumulated set, so ordering is disturbed while downward closure is not.')]));

B.push(h2('14.  The Ideal is All-or-Nothing by Column'));
B.push(p([t('The down-set property can be sharpened considerably. Group the admissible cells into columns indexed by (n, ℓ), each of capacity 2(2ℓ+1), and record for each column how many of its cells are occupied. Of the twenty-five columns of Λ, nineteen are occupied to their full capacity and six are entirely empty. None is partially filled.')]));
B.push(img('F11_columns.png',640,206));
B.push(cap(10,'The column structure of the realised ideal. (a) Occupancy against capacity for each of the twenty-five (n, ℓ) columns: nineteen saturated, six empty, none partial. (b) The column poset, with occupied columns shaded and the maximal occupied columns outlined; the realised system is a down-set of this twenty-five element poset. (c) All 120 down-sets of the column poset, by the number of lattice cells each spans. The realised system spans 118 cells and sits at the 79th percentile — among the larger configurations available, though not the largest.'));
B.push(p([t('The reason is again the filling process. A subshell fills to its Pauli capacity before the next begins, so a partially filled column corresponds only to the transient state between two consecutive elements and never to the accumulated set. Configurations such as 3d⁶ exist as the ground state of iron, but by the time the accumulation reaches zinc the 3d column is complete, and it remains complete thereafter.')]));
B.push(p([t('This is a strictly stronger statement than downward closure alone. A general order ideal of Λ may assign any height to each column subject to the closure condition; the realised ideal assigns only zero or capacity. The realised system is therefore determined by a binary choice per column, and the admissible configurations are exactly the down-sets of the twenty-five element column poset. There are 120 of these. The periodic system is one, spanning 118 cells, which places it at the 79th percentile of the 120 by size — among the larger configurations available, though six columns remain unoccupied.')]));

B.push(h2('15.  Robustness of the Occupied Set'));
B.push(p([t('Both properties of Sections 13 and 14 are stated for ground-state configurations. A natural objection is that they might be artifacts of that restriction, since an excited atom occupies configurations no ground state does. The objection can be tested, and it does not survive.')]));
B.push(p([t('Three constructions of an excited-state occupied set were built and compared with the ground-state set. The first admits every subshell reached by a documented low-lying excitation of any element; the second admits every filled subshell of every atom, inner shells included; the third admits the valence cell of each element\'s lowest documented excited configuration. All three return the same 118 cells. The union with the ground-state set is the ground-state set.')]));
B.push(p([t('The reason is structural rather than accidental. An excited configuration promotes an electron into a subshell that the aufbau sequence reaches at slightly higher atomic number, and the periodic system already contains those elements. Sodium excited from 3s to 3p occupies a cell that aluminium through argon hold as ground states; barium excited from 6s to 5d occupies a cell held from lanthanum onward. Excitation moves an atom to a cell that some other element already occupies. The second construction makes this vivid: aufbau walks each column from k = 1 to capacity one electron at a time, and every cell it passes through is the valence cell of some element.')]));
B.push(p([t('One extension does change the set. A singly-excited Rydberg atom, promoted to high n, reaches cells (n, ℓ, 1) for any admissible (n, ℓ), including all six of the empty columns identified in Section 14. Admitting these gives 124 cells rather than 118. The enlarged set remains an order ideal, but it is no longer all-or-nothing: the six new columns stand at height one against capacities of 10 to 18. This is a clean separation of the two properties. Downward closure is robust to excitation; saturation is specifically a fact about ground states.')]));
B.push(p([t('A related correction belongs here. An earlier draft of this work asserted that actinides with 5f ground states reach the 5g subshell under excitation, and hence that the g-layer is populated. Lattice admissibility is not in question, since (5, 4, k) satisfies ℓ ≤ n−1. But no neutral actinide has a documented low-lying 5g configuration: the g orbital has a large centrifugal barrier and negligible core penetration, making it hydrogen-like and energetically remote in neutral atoms, and 5g occupancy is predicted to begin near Z ≈ 121. The earlier claim conflated admissibility with accessibility, and is withdrawn. The six columns are empty because element synthesis has not passed Z = 118, not because of any capacity limit — every occupied column is full, so Pauli capacity is nowhere the binding constraint.')]));

B.push(h2('16.  The Discovery Frontier'));
B.push(p([t('Because the occupied set is an ideal, it can grow only by acquiring a cell that covers one of its maximal elements. Those number exactly three: (5, 3, 14) held by lawrencium, (6, 2, 10) held by copernicium, and (7, 1, 6) held by oganesson. There are five cells covering them — (5, 4, 14), (6, 3, 10), (6, 3, 14), (7, 2, 6) and (7, 2, 10) — and any element beyond those presently known must occupy one of these addresses in configuration space, or else extend the lattice beyond n = 7.')]));
B.push(p([t('In the column description of Section 14 the same statement reads more simply: growth is the filling of whole columns rather than the accumulation of individual cells, and the five covering cells are the entry points to three of the six empty columns.')]));
B.push(p([t('The limits of this claim require emphasis, and they are the same limits Section 3 places on the reserved cells. The claim constrains valence configuration only. It says nothing about nuclear stability, synthesis cross-sections, half-lives, or whether a nucleus placed at such an address would persist long enough to be observed, all of which are governed by nuclear structure rather than by electron bookkeeping. The statement is that if a new element is characterised, its valence configuration will be found at one of five coordinates — not that any of the five will be reached. An earlier draft claimed that the framework transforms the search for superheavy elements into a bounded problem; that claim overstates what valence bookkeeping can deliver and is withdrawn.')]));
B.push(p([t('The same machinery applied retrospectively has modest content. Taking the elements known in 1869 and asking which admissible cells lie below an occupied cell but are themselves unoccupied flags thirteen addresses, and all three of Mendeleev\'s celebrated predictions — eka-boron, eka-aluminium and eka-silicon — are among them, robustly across several reconstructions of the 1869 list. But the flag is not selective: it also flags the noble gases, which Mendeleev did not predict and whose discovery in the 1890s was a genuine surprise, and it does not rank its thirteen candidates. The ideal property is a completeness check, a necessary condition on the occupied set, rather than a prediction engine. Mendeleev did considerably better with chemical reasoning, and did it in 1869.')]));

B.push(h2('17.  The Adjacency Result'));
B.push(p([t('One consequence of the coordinate change deserves separate statement because it is the most visually direct. Consider ytterbium at (4, 3, 13) and platinum at (5, 2, 8). In the conventional table these elements are maximally separated by the layout: Yb sits in the detached lanthanide strip printed beneath the body of the table, Pt sits in period 6 of the main block, and nothing in the printed arrangement suggests a relationship between them.')]));
B.push(p([t('In the lattice they are close. Both occupy the high-k region of the high-ℓ layers, separated by one step in ℓ and a few in k. The late f-block and the late 5d metals form a contiguous region that the two-dimensional layout tears in half.')]));
B.push(img('F09_adjacency.png',640,245));
B.push(cap(11,'The adjacency the conventional layout conceals. (a) In the standard table, ytterbium is displaced into the lanthanide strip, far from platinum; the dashed arc indicates the separation imposed by the layout. (b) In the (ℓ, k) projection of the lattice, Yb and Pt are neighbours within a contiguous region of high configuration-space distance. Marker size encodes n.'));
B.push(p([t('This adjacency is a geometric fact about Λ and requires no further justification. Whether it carries chemical significance is a separate question, and the postscript records one attempt to exploit it that did not succeed.')]));

