// ═══════════════════ PART IV ═══════════════════
B.push(new Paragraph({children:[new PageBreak()]}));
B.push(h1('PART IV — THE LATTICE AMONG SORTING SYSTEMS'));
B.push(p([t('Prefatory note. ',{bold:true}),t('Three established traditions order the chemical elements, each anchored in a different class of measurement. This part characterises them, compares each with Λ, and establishes the relation that most sharply distinguishes the lattice from all of them. The central result is order-theoretic and, unlike the empirical comparisons of Part V, admits an exact answer.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:200}}));

B.push(h2('18.  Three Sorting Paradigms'));
B.push(h3('18.1  Quantum sorting'));
B.push(p([t('Quantum sorting orders elements by the quantum numbers of the ground-state configuration, anchored observationally in spectroscopy — term symbols, ionisation thresholds, orbital angular momentum. Its canonical forms are the Madelung rule of Section 11, filling by increasing n + ℓ with ties broken by increasing n, and Janet\'s left-step arrangement, which blocks by constant n + ℓ. Both are ordinal constructions on (n, ℓ, k), and both produce a total order on the elements.')]));
B.push(p([t('Measured against atomic number, Madelung ordering achieves Kendall τ = 0.90 with twenty-eight inversions. Those inversions do not coincide with the twenty documented anomalous ground-state configurations: only ten elements appear in both sets, twenty are misplaced by Madelung despite regular configurations, and ten have anomalous configurations yet are correctly placed. Misplacement by the filling rule and anomaly in the configuration are largely independent phenomena, and a framework that explained one would not thereby explain the other.')]));
B.push(h3('18.2  Structural sorting'));
B.push(p([t('Structural sorting orders elements so that observed crystal structures separate cleanly. Pettifor\'s Mendeleev number, introduced in 1984, was constructed empirically from binary AB structure maps and optimised so that chemically similar elements occupy adjacent positions; Villars\' Periodic Number emphasises valence-electron count with rows corresponding to the principal quantum number; more recent variants derive comparable scales from substitution-probability matrices mined from the Inorganic Crystal Structure Database or from ab initio calculation. The measurement basis is the structure database itself.')]));
B.push(p([t('Villars and co-workers make a claim that bears directly on the present work: that atomic number, group number and the principal quantum numbers taken by themselves lose the information the Mendeleev scales retain. That claim is testable against Λ and is confirmed. Regressing Pettifor\'s MN on atomic number alone gives a cross-validated R² of −0.007, literally worse than predicting the mean. Regressing it on the three lattice coordinates linearly gives −0.011, no better. But adding a single quadratic term in ℓ raises the cross-validated R² to 0.515, and the gain survives a permutation test at p < 0.0001. The information Villars describes as lost is recoverable from Λ\'s coordinates — but not linearly, and Section 20 shows that the non-linearity is unavoidable.')]));
B.push(h3('18.3  Integral sorting'));
B.push(p([t('Integral sorting orders elements by integer counts of nucleons: atomic number Z, established by Moseley\'s X-ray measurements in 1913, and mass number A, the basis of Mendeleev\'s original arrangement by atomic weight.')]));
B.push(p([t('The two agree closely, at Kendall τ = 0.997 across the elements with settled mass data, and the disagreements are exactly four: argon with potassium, cobalt with nickel, selenium with bromine, and tellurium with iodine. These are precisely the pairs Mendeleev transposed against atomic weight on chemical grounds, and the computation recovers them without being directed to look. Integral sorting by weight and by charge differ at four places, all of them known since 1913, and the recovery serves here as a validation of the method rather than as a new result.')]));
B.push(p([t('Nuclear structure, by contrast, is invisible to the lattice. The proton magic numbers 2, 8, 20, 28, 50 and 82 occupy lattice ranks 3, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 13, a mean of 7.67 against 11.03 for all elements, with p = 0.056 by a one-sample test. That is not significant at conventional thresholds, and the sample of six is in any case too small to be conclusive; but the direction of the result is unsurprising, since Λ encodes electron configuration while magic numbers are nucleon shell closures. No relationship should be expected, and none is found.')]));

B.push(tcap(1,'The four sorting systems compared. Only Λ produces a partial order; the others are total orders on the elements.'));
B.push(table(
  ['System','Sort key','Measurement basis','Order type','τ vs Z'],
  [['Quantum — Madelung','n+ℓ, then n','spectroscopy','total','0.90'],
   ['Quantum — Janet','blocks of n+ℓ','spectroscopy','total (blocked)','—'],
   ['Structural — Pettifor','empirical scale','ICSD structure maps','total','−0.23'],
   ['Structural — Villars','periodic number','compound formation','total','—'],
   ['Integral — Z','nuclear charge','Moseley X-ray spectra','total','1.00'],
   ['Integral — A','nucleon count','mass spectrometry','total','0.997'],
   ['Lach Λ','(n, ℓ, k) triple','definitional','PARTIAL','0.50 (rank)']],
  [2000,1700,2100,1500,1000],['Lach Λ']));
B.push(p([t('The table records the single largest structural difference. Every prior system produces a total order — a sequence from 1 to 118 — whereas Λ produces a partial order, leaving 46 per cent of element pairs incomparable. This is why the apparatus of Parts II and III exists at all: a total order has order dimension 1, no non-trivial antichains and no interesting ideals, so the Sperner property, the down-set structure and Birkhoff representation are available for Λ and undefined for the others. Λ is not a competitor to these schemes. Where they rank, it relates.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:120}}));

B.push(h2('19.  Linear Extensions: The Central Comparison'));
B.push(p([t('Section 7 established that Λ has order dimension three, which is to say that it is the intersection of three linear orders. That makes one question natural above all others: are any of the established sorting systems among them?')]));
B.push(p([t('A ranking of the elements is order-preserving on Λ, and hence a linear extension of it, when a ≤ b in the lattice implies rank(a) ≤ rank(b). Testing this exhaustively over all 3,742 comparable pairs among the 118 occupied cells gives a decisive answer.')]));
B.push(eq('(n, ℓ, k) ≤ (n′, ℓ′, k′)  componentwise   ⟹   Z ≤ Z′','13'));
B.push(p([t('Atomic number reverses none of the 3,742 pairs. Madelung order likewise reverses none. Pettifor\'s Mendeleev number reverses 960 of the 1,684 comparable pairs among the elements for which it is defined — 57 per cent, which is worse than chance — and in the reversed orientation still reverses 723, or 43 per cent. Neither orientation is a linear extension.')]));
B.push(img('F12_linext.png',640,248));
B.push(cap(12,'Order compatibility of the sorting systems with Λ. (a) The three verdicts. Atomic number and Madelung order reverse none of the 3,742 comparable pairs; Pettifor\'s scale reverses 960 of 1,684. (b) Rank difference across a sample of comparable pairs: points below the line reverse the lattice order. (c) The obstruction to linearity, ∂MN/∂k by block — positive in the p- and d-blocks, negative in the f-block.'));
B.push(p([t('Equation (13) admits a direct proof. Aufbau adds electrons monotonically, so reaching a cell with larger shell, larger subshell and larger occupancy requires strictly more electrons; the componentwise order therefore refines into the atomic-number order. The converse fails: 3,161 of the 6,903 element pairs are incomparable in Λ. The lattice is thus a strict coarsening of atomic number — it orders fewer pairs, but never contradicts Z where it does order them.')]));
B.push(p([t('The consequences are threefold, and they reframe results reported elsewhere in this paper. First, Λ\'s order is compatible with quantum and integral sorting and incompatible with structural sorting; the three traditions do not stand in a common relation to the lattice. Second, this explains rather than merely records the benchmark defeat of Section 24: asking Λ to predict a quantity aligned with Pettifor\'s scale is asking a partial order to reproduce a ranking that reverses it, and losing was structurally guaranteed. Third, the dimension result of Section 7 acquires content. Λ requires three linear extensions to reconstruct; two natural ones are now identified. What a natural third might be, or whether one exists, is a well-posed question this paper does not answer.')]));

B.push(h2('20.  The Obstruction to Linearity'));
B.push(p([t('Section 18.2 reported that Pettifor\'s scale is recoverable from Λ\'s coordinates non-linearly but not linearly. That is not an empirical finding about the difficulty of the fit; it is a theorem, and the proof is short.')]));
B.push(p([t('A linear function F(n, ℓ, k) = a + bn + cℓ + dk has ∂F/∂k = d, a constant independent of ℓ. Equivalently, its mixed second difference vanishes:')]));
B.push(eq('Δ_ℓ Δ_k F  =  F(ℓ+1,k+1) − F(ℓ+1,k) − F(ℓ,k+1) + F(ℓ,k)  =  0','14'));
B.push(p([t('The measured slopes of MN against k, computed within each block, are +4.36 ± 0.55 in the p-block, +4.28 ± 0.43 in the d-block, and −1.47 ± 0.35 in the f-block. The slope changes sign, so no constant d can serve, and no search over coefficients can succeed. The best possible linear fit, which ordinary least squares attains by construction, has R² = 0.111 with a residual standard deviation of 27.7 on a scale spanning 101 units. That number is a ceiling, not a result.')]));
B.push(p([t('The reversal has a chemical explanation. Pettifor ordered elements so that chemically similar ones sit adjacent. Across the lanthanides, similarity tracks ionic radius, which decreases through the series by the lanthanide contraction while occupancy increases; across the d-block, similarity tracks d-electron count directly. The coordinate k therefore has a consistent definition but an inconsistent relation to chemical similarity — aligned in the d-block, anti-aligned in the f-block. The quadratic term in ℓ that repairs the regression is a smooth stand-in for that discrete reversal.')]));
B.push(p([t('Relaxing linearity to additive separability, F ≈ f(n) + g(ℓ) + h(k) with the three functions arbitrary, raises the cross-validated R² to 0.69. This looks like progress and is not. Additive models also have ∂F/∂k independent of ℓ and so also cannot represent a sign flip; what they exploit instead is a confound in the design. Occupancies k ≥ 11 occur only at ℓ = 3, so the parameters governing those values are fitted from lanthanides alone and reproduce them exactly — erbium, thulium, ytterbium and lutetium have residuals of precisely zero. The design cannot distinguish the hypothesis that k behaves differently in the f-block from the hypothesis that high occupancies simply have low Mendeleev numbers, and the additive model\'s apparent success is a measure of that confounding rather than of separability.')]));

B.push(h2('21.  Relation to Existing Work'));
B.push(p([t('Alternative periodic arrangements have a long history — helical, cylindrical, pyramidal and left-step forms among them. These generally reorganise by atomic number and chemical periodicity while preserving a two-dimensional surface. The present construction differs in treating the three quantum numbers as independent geometric axes and, more consequentially, in producing a partial rather than a total order.')]));
B.push(p([t('Order theory has an established presence in chemistry, and two lines of work stand close enough to require explicit comparison. Leal and Restrepo have developed a formal account of the periodic system as an ordered hypergraph, with elements as vertices, chemical similarity classes as hyperedges, and an order relation induced by measured properties such as atomic number or electronegativity. Their construction and the present one order different objects by different relations: theirs orders elements by properties, the present work orders configurations by componentwise comparison of quantum numbers. The two are complementary. A remark of Restrepo\'s is worth recording — that the several periodic tables in circulation may be understood as projections of a single underlying structure. Section 11.2 arrives at a particular instance of that idea from the opposite direction, obtaining the Janet arrangement as the projection of Λ along n + ℓ.')]));
B.push(p([t('Quintero, Brüggemann and Restrepo have separately introduced what they term posetic coordinates, assigning to each object of a poset the triple of cardinalities of its up-set, its down-set and its incomparable set, and mapping those triples into ternary and FOU plots. Despite the shared vocabulary, the notion of dimension employed there is not the order dimension of Section 7: their mappings are visualisation devices reducing a Hasse diagram to a readable plot, whereas order dimension is an intrinsic invariant counting the linear orders needed to reconstruct the relation. Their coordinates describe an object\'s position within a given poset; the coordinates of the present work define the poset. The two notions are independent, and the results of Section 7 neither follow from nor conflict with theirs. Their machinery is nonetheless applicable to Λ and would answer a question this paper does not address — where the 118 occupied cells sit as characterised by their up-set, down-set and incomparability profiles — and that is noted as a natural continuation.')]));
B.push(p([t('Whether the particular results of Parts II and III are new to the literature has not been established. A limited search located the works discussed above and no closer prior art, but dimension theory alone is the subject of a substantial monograph literature, and the possibility that componentwise-ordered configuration posets have been treated elsewhere cannot be excluded on the evidence gathered here. The results are offered as correct and checkable rather than as novel, and readers in a position to identify precedent are encouraged to do so.')]));

// ═══════════════════ PART V ═══════════════════
B.push(new Paragraph({children:[new PageBreak()]}));
B.push(h1('PART V — LIMITS'));
B.push(p([t('Prefatory note. ',{bold:true}),t('This part sets out what the lattice cannot do. It is given at the same length and the same standard of evidence as the positive results, for two reasons. A boundary established by computation is a result in the same sense that a theorem is, and several of the limits recorded here foreclose lines of attack that the earlier parts would otherwise invite. Every predictive application attempted in the course of this work is reported, including those that failed, with the diagnosis in each case.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:200}}));

B.push(h2('22.  The Metric Obstruction'));
B.push(h3('22.1  Λ is a subset of ℕ³'));
B.push(p([t('Every axis of the lattice is a count. The principal quantum number enumerates radial nodes, the azimuthal number enumerates angular nodes, and k enumerates electrons. Λ is therefore a subset of ℕ³, and every quantity constructed from its coordinates — distances, displacements, rank functionals, any index whatever — is a pure number.')]));
B.push(p([t('This is not a gap awaiting repair. It is constitutive of the construction, and it bounds what the lattice can be asked to do. Any application requiring a dimensional prediction — an energy, a rate, a length — must import its scale from outside, and whatever success follows belongs to the imported quantity rather than to Λ.')]));
B.push(img('F15_limits.png',640,206));
B.push(cap(13,'The limits of the lattice. (a) Each axis is a count, so Λ ⊆ ℕ³ and every derived quantity is dimensionless. (b) The metric-axis dilemma: a fourth coordinate independent of (n, ℓ, k) carries no lattice information, while one that is a function of them turns the admissible set into a functional graph and destroys closure, with 14,293 failures of each kind. (c) Every predictive application attempted in this work, with the established baseline marked where one exists.'));
B.push(h3('22.2  No metric coordinate can be adjoined'));
B.push(p([t('The obvious response to Section 22.1 is to add a fourth axis carrying metric information — an orbital energy, a screening parameter, a Slater–Condon integral. The proposal fails, and for a reason sharper than the dimensionlessness itself.')]));
B.push(p([t('Any metric quantity worth adding is a function of the existing coordinates; that is precisely what makes it informative about the lattice. But a coordinate determined by the others is not an independent axis of a product order. Adjoining it turns Λ from a product region into the graph of a function, and functional graphs are not sublattices of the ambient product. Direct test with a discretised energy proxy confirms the consequence: 14,293 join failures and 14,293 meet failures out of the pairs available.')]));
B.push(p([t('The choice is therefore stark. A metric axis independent of (n, ℓ, k) carries no information about the lattice and is decorative. A metric axis dependent on (n, ℓ, k) destroys the closure on which every result of Part II rests. This obstruction is distinct from that of Section 22.1 and arguably sharper: it bites even when a metric quantity is supplied free of charge.')]));
B.push(p([t('What remains available is labelling rather than coordinatisation. Metric data may be attached to lattice cells, with Λ serving as an index structure and the metric content coming from outside. Section 23 reports what happens when that is done.')]));

B.push(h2('23.  Applications Attempted'));
B.push(p([t('Five predictive applications were attempted. All five failed or were outperformed. They are reported together, with diagnoses, because the pattern across them is more informative than any one.')]));
B.push(h3('23.1  Hydride formation enthalpy'));
B.push(p([t('The enthalpy of hydride formation is measured, tabulated, dimensional, independent of this framework, and governs whether a metal absorbs hydrogen at all. Values for thirty-nine transition metals, lanthanides and actinides give')]));
B.push(eq('ΔH_f  =  +136.2  −  89.5·ℓ  +  6.4·k        (kJ · mol⁻¹ H)','15'));
B.push(p([t('with both coefficients significant — ℓ at p = 3.1 × 10⁻⁸ and k at p = 8.1 × 10⁻⁴ — an in-sample R² of 0.592 and a leave-one-out cross-validated R² of 0.514. Adding n, the configuration-space distance, or higher terms does not improve the cross-validated fit. Atomic number alone gives a negative cross-validated R², carrying no predictive information on this data.')]));
B.push(img('F13_hydride.png',640,243));
B.push(cap(14,'The lattice coordinates against hydride formation enthalpy for 39 metals. (a) Measured versus predicted for Equation (15); the dashed line is parity. (b) Variance explained by candidate models, in-sample and cross-validated. (c) Permutation null for the contribution of k: the observed gain of 0.15 in R² lies outside the distribution from 10,000 shuffles, at p = 0.0004.'));
B.push(p([t('Taken alone this looks encouraging. It should not be taken alone. Two schemes have addressed this problem for decades, and benchmarking against them under identical cross-validation is unflattering.')]));
B.push(img('F14_benchmark.png',640,223));
B.push(cap(15,'The lattice weighed against prior art. (a) Cross-validated R² for each scheme, with the number of fitted predictors marked. Pettifor\'s single-variable scale outperforms the two-variable lattice model by a wide margin, and Miedema\'s full form outperforms both. (b) Absolute correlation of the lattice coordinates with the established descriptors: the block index ℓ is strongly correlated with all three, whereas the occupancy k is uncorrelated with all three. (c) Cross-validated R² of each established scheme before and after adding k, with permutation significance.'));
B.push(tcap(2,'Prediction of hydride formation enthalpy across 39 metals, all schemes fitted and cross-validated identically. Paired Wilcoxon tests compare squared leave-one-out errors against the lattice model.'));
B.push(table(
  ['Scheme','Predictors','Fit R²','CV R²','CV RMSE','vs. lattice'],
  [['Miedema, full form','4','0.934','0.895','18.1','p < 0.0001'],
   ['Pettifor, MN + MN²','2','0.854','0.828','23.2','p < 0.0001'],
   ['Miedema, φ* + n_ws','2','0.842','0.808','24.6','p = 0.0001'],
   ['Pettifor, MN alone','1','0.808','0.790','25.7','p = 0.0003'],
   ['Lattice, ℓ + n + k','3','0.608','0.504','39.4','—'],
   ['Lattice, ℓ + k','2','0.592','0.514','39.1','—'],
   ['Atomic number Z','1','0.028','−0.076','58.1','p < 0.0001']],
  [2300,1300,1000,1000,1100,1500],['Lattice, ℓ + k']));
B.push(p([t('The result runs against the framework and is stated plainly. Pettifor\'s scale, using a single variable, achieves a cross-validated R² of 0.790 against the lattice\'s 0.514 with two; Miedema\'s full form reaches 0.895. Paired Wilcoxon tests reject equality against every established scheme, the weakest at p = 0.0003. Nor does combination rescue the position: adding ℓ and k to the full Miedema model changes its cross-validated R² from 0.895 to 0.888, a decrease.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:120}}));
B.push(p([t('One result survives, and it is worth isolating precisely because the rest did not. The two lattice coordinates behave quite differently against the established descriptors. The block index ℓ correlates strongly with all of them, at absolute values between 0.68 and 0.77, which is expected since ℓ is the block label and every chemical scale encodes block membership. The occupancy k is uncorrelated with all three — r = −0.05 against the Mendeleev number, +0.14 against Miedema\'s φ*, −0.13 against n_ws — none approaching significance. Adding k to Pettifor\'s Mendeleev number raises cross-validated R² from 0.790 to 0.844, a gain significant at p = 0.001 by permutation, with smaller but significant gains for the quadratic Pettifor form and the two-parameter Miedema form. Against the full four-parameter Miedema model the gain vanishes. The defensible claim is narrow: valence subshell occupancy carries information about hydride stability that Pettifor\'s chemical scale does not encode, and appending it improves that scale measurably. This is a contribution to an existing method, not an alternative to it.')]));
B.push(p([t('A related result belongs here. The classification by ΔQ direction from Section 6, derived purely from the angular-momentum constraint with no reference to any measurement, separates the same data: Class C elements average −61.9 kJ · mol⁻¹ H against −15.7 for Class B, significant at p = 0.010 by Welch\'s test. It is the only instance in this work of a purely geometric construction tracking a measured quantity without assistance.')]));
B.push(h3('23.2  Anomalous ground-state configurations'));
B.push(p([t('Twenty elements have documented ground states departing from strict aufbau filling. If the lattice carried information about them, anomalous elements should be enriched at structurally distinguished positions. They are not. Anomalous elements sit at half-filled subshells in 10 per cent of cases against a baseline of 16 per cent, at filled subshells in 5 per cent against 16 per cent, and on the kainosymmetric boundary in 20 per cent against 27 per cent. Every enrichment runs the wrong way.')]));
B.push(p([t('The diagnosis is instructive. Anomalies arise from competition between promotion energy and exchange stabilisation, a metric effect, and Section 22 establishes that the lattice cannot see metric effects. The point is sharpened by the false positives of any lattice-based rule: tungsten at (5, 2, 4) is regular while molybdenum at (4, 2, 4) is anomalous, and the two differ only in n. The distinction between them is relativistic contraction of the 6s orbital, which scales with Z² and is invisible to a coordinate that records only which shell is occupied.')]));
B.push(h3('23.3  Exchange-energy labelling'));
B.push(p([t('Section 22.2 leaves open the labelling of lattice cells with metric data. This was tried for the anomaly problem. Hund\'s first rule gives the number of parallel-spin pairs in a subshell directly from (ℓ, k), so the exchange stabilisation available on promoting an electron is computable from lattice coordinates alone. Labelling every cell with its exchange-pair count and testing whether the resulting gain separates anomalous from normal elements gives no separation: areas under the receiver-operating curve cluster around 0.5 across every variant tried, the best reaching 0.589 at p = 0.23. The textbook half-filled and one-below-full heuristic achieves precision 0.42 and recall 0.26. The exchange calculation is correct; what it lacks is the promotion cost, which is not a function of lattice position.')]));
B.push(h3('23.4  Ionisation energy and electronegativity'));
B.push(p([t('First ionisation energy is predicted at cross-validated R² = 0.42, but only by importing the hydrogenic 1/n² dependence, which is external physics rather than lattice structure; the lattice coordinates alone reach 0.19. Electronegativity is essentially unpredicted at 0.12. Both are recorded as attempted and unsuccessful.')]));
B.push(h3('23.5  What the pattern shows'));
B.push(p([t('Across five applications the pattern is consistent. Structural questions about Λ — its dimension, closure, invariants, ideals, correspondences — yield results, and several are exact. Metric questions do not, and the failures are diagnosable in each case to the same cause. The lattice organises; it does not predict. That is a limitation, and it is also a reasonably precise characterisation of what kind of object Λ is.')]));

B.push(h2('24.  Conclusion'));
B.push(p([t('The construction of Part I is a change of coordinates and nothing more: the Pauli principle and the angular-momentum condition, written as constraints on three integer axes. What it produces is a partial order, and the substance of this paper is an account of that order.')]));
B.push(p([t('Part II contains the results of which this work can be most confident, because they are structural facts checkable by direct computation rather than correlations with data. The order dimension of Λ is exactly three, so the third coordinate is necessary and not a convenience. The admissible cells form a bounded distributive lattice whose unique minimum is hydrogen, and closure follows from a standard sublattice argument that also shows why the hydrogenic labelling cannot replace the occupancy coordinate. Shell capacities emerge as slice volumes for n ≤ 5; the lattice is Sperner without being rank-symmetric; its rank generating function vanishes at q = −1, an exact parity balance traceable to spin. The Madelung rule is a linear functional whose level sets pair to give the period lengths, and projection along it reproduces Janet\'s left-step table exactly, while fibration along the n-axis reproduces the chemical groups and places helium among the alkaline earths by a second and independent route. The kainosymmetric subshells occupy precisely the boundary diagonal. And the orientation of the lattice is rigid: exactly one of 120 subshell orderings survives, and the Madelung functional is essentially unique among small-integer shears.')]));
B.push(p([t('Part III locates the periodic system within that structure. The 118 occupied cells form an order ideal, a property inherited from the monotone character of aufbau filling and robust to every excited-state extension that could be constructed. The ideal is all-or-nothing by column, making the realised system one of 120 admissible configurations rather than one of a far larger family, and leaving three maximal cells with five covering addresses for any further element.')]));
B.push(p([t('Part IV establishes the relation that most sharply characterises the lattice. Atomic number and Madelung order are linear extensions of Λ, reversing none of its comparable pairs; Pettifor\'s scale is not order-compatible in either orientation. Λ is a coarsening of atomic number that never contradicts it, and is orthogonal to structural sorting. The obstruction to a linear relation between Λ and the Pettifor scale is a theorem, not a difficulty of fitting.')]));
B.push(p([t('Part V sets the limits. Λ ⊆ ℕ³, so every derived quantity is dimensionless, and no metric coordinate can be adjoined without destroying the closure that the positive results depend on. Five predictive applications were attempted and none succeeded competitively. One narrow empirical result survives benchmarking: the occupancy coordinate carries information about hydride stability that established chemical scales do not encode, and improves Pettifor\'s scale measurably.')]));
B.push(p([t('Read together, the parts describe a combinatorial object with genuine order-theoretic structure, a definite and in places exact relationship to existing periodic formalisms, modest empirical purchase through one of its coordinates, and no reach into metric or nuclear physics. The field equation from which this framework originally took its name is, on the evidence assembled here, its least substantial component. The lattice is the contribution, and the paper is named for it.')]));

display_content.code:
// ═══════════════════ PART IV ═══════════════════
B.push(new Paragraph({children:[new PageBreak()]}));
B.push(h1('PART IV — THE LATTICE AMONG SORTING SYSTEMS'));
B.push(p([t('Prefatory note. ',{bold:true}),t('Three established traditions order the chemical elements, each anchored in a different class of measurement. This part characterises them, compares each with Λ, and establishes the relation that most sharply distinguishes the lattice from all of them. The central result is order-theoretic and, unlike the empirical comparisons of Part V, admits an exact answer.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:200}}));

B.push(h2('18.  Three Sorting Paradigms'));
B.push(h3('18.1  Quantum sorting'));
B.push(p([t('Quantum sorting orders elements by the quantum numbers of the ground-state configuration, anchored observationally in spectroscopy — term symbols, ionisation thresholds, orbital angular momentum. Its canonical forms are the Madelung rule of Section 11, filling by increasing n + ℓ with ties broken by increasing n, and Janet\'s left-step arrangement, which blocks by constant n + ℓ. Both are ordinal constructions on (n, ℓ, k), and both produce a total order on the elements.')]));
B.push(p([t('Measured against atomic number, Madelung ordering achieves Kendall τ = 0.90 with twenty-eight inversions. Those inversions do not coincide with the twenty documented anomalous ground-state configurations: only ten elements appear in both sets, twenty are misplaced by Madelung despite regular configurations, and ten have anomalous configurations yet are correctly placed. Misplacement by the filling rule and anomaly in the configuration are largely independent phenomena, and a framework that explained one would not thereby explain the other.')]));
B.push(h3('18.2  Structural sorting'));
B.push(p([t('Structural sorting orders elements so that observed crystal structures separate cleanly. Pettifor\'s Mendeleev number, introduced in 1984, was constructed empirically from binary AB structure maps and optimised so that chemically similar elements occupy adjacent positions; Villars\' Periodic Number emphasises valence-electron count with rows corresponding to the principal quantum number; more recent variants derive comparable scales from substitution-probability matrices mined from the Inorganic Crystal Structure Database or from ab initio calculation. The measurement basis is the structure database itself.')]));
B.push(p([t('Villars and co-workers make a claim that bears directly on the present work: that atomic number, group number and the principal quantum numbers taken by themselves lose the information the Mendeleev scales retain. That claim is testable against Λ and is confirmed. Regressing Pettifor\'s MN on atomic number alone gives a cross-validated R² of −0.007, literally worse than predicting the mean. Regressing it on the three lattice coordinates linearly gives −0.011, no better. But adding a single quadratic term in ℓ raises the cross-validated R² to 0.515, and the gain survives a permutation test at p < 0.0001. The information Villars describes as lost is recoverable from Λ\'s coordinates — but not linearly, and Section 20 shows that the non-linearity is unavoidable.')]));
B.push(h3('18.3  Integral sorting'));
B.push(p([t('Integral sorting orders elements by integer counts of nucleons: atomic number Z, established by Moseley\'s X-ray measurements in 1913, and mass number A, the basis of Mendeleev\'s original arrangement by atomic weight.')]));
B.push(p([t('The two agree closely, at Kendall τ = 0.997 across the elements with settled mass data, and the disagreements are exactly four: argon with potassium, cobalt with nickel, selenium with bromine, and tellurium with iodine. These are precisely the pairs Mendeleev transposed against atomic weight on chemical grounds, and the computation recovers them without being directed to look. Integral sorting by weight and by charge differ at four places, all of them known since 1913, and the recovery serves here as a validation of the method rather than as a new result.')]));
B.push(p([t('Nuclear structure, by contrast, is invisible to the lattice. The proton magic numbers 2, 8, 20, 28, 50 and 82 occupy lattice ranks 3, 6, 7, 8, 9 and 13, a mean of 7.67 against 11.03 for all elements, with p = 0.056 by a one-sample test. That is not significant at conventional thresholds, and the sample of six is in any case too small to be conclusive; but the direction of the result is unsurprising, since Λ encodes electron configuration while magic numbers are nucleon shell closures. No relationship should be expected, and none is found.')]));

B.push(tcap(1,'The four sorting systems compared. Only Λ produces a partial order; the others are total orders on the elements.'));
B.push(table(
  ['System','Sort key','Measurement basis','Order type','τ vs Z'],
  [['Quantum — Madelung','n+ℓ, then n','spectroscopy','total','0.90'],
   ['Quantum — Janet','blocks of n+ℓ','spectroscopy','total (blocked)','—'],
   ['Structural — Pettifor','empirical scale','ICSD structure maps','total','−0.23'],
   ['Structural — Villars','periodic number','compound formation','total','—'],
   ['Integral — Z','nuclear charge','Moseley X-ray spectra','total','1.00'],
   ['Integral — A','nucleon count','mass spectrometry','total','0.997'],
   ['Lach Λ','(n, ℓ, k) triple','definitional','PARTIAL','0.50 (rank)']],
  [2000,1700,2100,1500,1000],['Lach Λ']));
B.push(p([t('The table records the single largest structural difference. Every prior system produces a total order — a sequence from 1 to 118 — whereas Λ produces a partial order, leaving 46 per cent of element pairs incomparable. This is why the apparatus of Parts II and III exists at all: a total order has order dimension 1, no non-trivial antichains and no interesting ideals, so the Sperner property, the down-set structure and Birkhoff representation are available for Λ and undefined for the others. Λ is not a competitor to these schemes. Where they rank, it relates.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:120}}));

B.push(h2('19.  Linear Extensions: The Central Comparison'));
B.push(p([t('Section 7 established that Λ has order dimension three, which is to say that it is the intersection of three linear orders. That makes one question natural above all others: are any of the established sorting systems among them?')]));
B.push(p([t('A ranking of the elements is order-preserving on Λ, and hence a linear extension of it, when a ≤ b in the lattice implies rank(a) ≤ rank(b). Testing this exhaustively over all 3,742 comparable pairs among the 118 occupied cells gives a decisive answer.')]));
B.push(eq('(n, ℓ, k) ≤ (n′, ℓ′, k′)  componentwise   ⟹   Z ≤ Z′','13'));
B.push(p([t('Atomic number reverses none of the 3,742 pairs. Madelung order likewise reverses none. Pettifor\'s Mendeleev number reverses 960 of the 1,684 comparable pairs among the elements for which it is defined — 57 per cent, which is worse than chance — and in the reversed orientation still reverses 723, or 43 per cent. Neither orientation is a linear extension.')]));
B.push(img('F12_linext.png',640,248));
B.push(cap(12,'Order compatibility of the sorting systems with Λ. (a) The three verdicts. Atomic number and Madelung order reverse none of the 3,742 comparable pairs; Pettifor\'s scale reverses 960 of 1,684. (b) Rank difference across a sample of comparable pairs: points below the line reverse the lattice order. (c) The obstruction to linearity, ∂MN/∂k by block — positive in the p- and d-blocks, negative in the f-block.'));
B.push(p([t('Equation (13) admits a direct proof. Aufbau adds electrons monotonically, so reaching a cell with larger shell, larger subshell and larger occupancy requires strictly more electrons; the componentwise order therefore refines into the atomic-number order. The converse fails: 3,161 of the 6,903 element pairs are incomparable in Λ. The lattice is thus a strict coarsening of atomic number — it orders fewer pairs, but never contradicts Z where it does order them.')]));
B.push(p([t('The consequences are threefold, and they reframe results reported elsewhere in this paper. First, Λ\'s order is compatible with quantum and integral sorting and incompatible with structural sorting; the three traditions do not stand in a common relation to the lattice. Second, this explains rather than merely records the benchmark defeat of Section 24: asking Λ to predict a quantity aligned with Pettifor\'s scale is asking a partial order to reproduce a ranking that reverses it, and losing was structurally guaranteed. Third, the dimension result of Section 7 acquires content. Λ requires three linear extensions to reconstruct; two natural ones are now identified. What a natural third might be, or whether one exists, is a well-posed question this paper does not answer.')]));

B.push(h2('20.  The Obstruction to Linearity'));
B.push(p([t('Section 18.2 reported that Pettifor\'s scale is recoverable from Λ\'s coordinates non-linearly but not linearly. That is not an empirical finding about the difficulty of the fit; it is a theorem, and the proof is short.')]));
B.push(p([t('A linear function F(n, ℓ, k) = a + bn + cℓ + dk has ∂F/∂k = d, a constant independent of ℓ. Equivalently, its mixed second difference vanishes:')]));
B.push(eq('Δ_ℓ Δ_k F  =  F(ℓ+1,k+1) − F(ℓ+1,k) − F(ℓ,k+1) + F(ℓ,k)  =  0','14'));
B.push(p([t('The measured slopes of MN against k, computed within each block, are +4.36 ± 0.55 in the p-block, +4.28 ± 0.43 in the d-block, and −1.47 ± 0.35 in the f-block. The slope changes sign, so no constant d can serve, and no search over coefficients can succeed. The best possible linear fit, which ordinary least squares attains by construction, has R² = 0.111 with a residual standard deviation of 27.7 on a scale spanning 101 units. That number is a ceiling, not a result.')]));
B.push(p([t('The reversal has a chemical explanation. Pettifor ordered elements so that chemically similar ones sit adjacent. Across the lanthanides, similarity tracks ionic radius, which decreases through the series by the lanthanide contraction while occupancy increases; across the d-block, similarity tracks d-electron count directly. The coordinate k therefore has a consistent definition but an inconsistent relation to chemical similarity — aligned in the d-block, anti-aligned in the f-block. The quadratic term in ℓ that repairs the regression is a smooth stand-in for that discrete reversal.')]));
B.push(p([t('Relaxing linearity to additive separability, F ≈ f(n) + g(ℓ) + h(k) with the three functions arbitrary, raises the cross-validated R² to 0.69. This looks like progress and is not. Additive models also have ∂F/∂k independent of ℓ and so also cannot represent a sign flip; what they exploit instead is a confound in the design. Occupancies k ≥ 11 occur only at ℓ = 3, so the parameters governing those values are fitted from lanthanides alone and reproduce them exactly — erbium, thulium, ytterbium and lutetium have residuals of precisely zero. The design cannot distinguish the hypothesis that k behaves differently in the f-block from the hypothesis that high occupancies simply have low Mendeleev numbers, and the additive model\'s apparent success is a measure of that confounding rather than of separability.')]));

B.push(h2('21.  Relation to Existing Work'));
B.push(p([t('Alternative periodic arrangements have a long history — helical, cylindrical, pyramidal and left-step forms among them. These generally reorganise by atomic number and chemical periodicity while preserving a two-dimensional surface. The present construction differs in treating the three quantum numbers as independent geometric axes and, more consequentially, in producing a partial rather than a total order.')]));
B.push(p([t('Order theory has an established presence in chemistry, and two lines of work stand close enough to require explicit comparison. Leal and Restrepo have developed a formal account of the periodic system as an ordered hypergraph, with elements as vertices, chemical similarity classes as hyperedges, and an order relation induced by measured properties such as atomic number or electronegativity. Their construction and the present one order different objects by different relations: theirs orders elements by properties, the present work orders configurations by componentwise comparison of quantum numbers. The two are complementary. A remark of Restrepo\'s is worth recording — that the several periodic tables in circulation may be understood as projections of a single underlying structure. Section 11.2 arrives at a particular instance of that idea from the opposite direction, obtaining the Janet arrangement as the projection of Λ along n + ℓ.')]));
B.push(p([t('Quintero, Brüggemann and Restrepo have separately introduced what they term posetic coordinates, assigning to each object of a poset the triple of cardinalities of its up-set, its down-set and its incomparable set, and mapping those triples into ternary and FOU plots. Despite the shared vocabulary, the notion of dimension employed there is not the order dimension of Section 7: their mappings are visualisation devices reducing a Hasse diagram to a readable plot, whereas order dimension is an intrinsic invariant counting the linear orders needed to reconstruct the relation. Their coordinates describe an object\'s position within a given poset; the coordinates of the present work define the poset. The two notions are independent, and the results of Section 7 neither follow from nor conflict with theirs. Their machinery is nonetheless applicable to Λ and would answer a question this paper does not address — where the 118 occupied cells sit as characterised by their up-set, down-set and incomparability profiles — and that is noted as a natural continuation.')]));
B.push(p([t('Whether the particular results of Parts II and III are new to the literature has not been established. A limited search located the works discussed above and no closer prior art, but dimension theory alone is the subject of a substantial monograph literature, and the possibility that componentwise-ordered configuration posets have been treated elsewhere cannot be excluded on the evidence gathered here. The results are offered as correct and checkable rather than as novel, and readers in a position to identify precedent are encouraged to do so.')]));

// ═══════════════════ PART V ═══════════════════
B.push(new Paragraph({children:[new PageBreak()]}));
B.push(h1('PART V — LIMITS'));
B.push(p([t('Prefatory note. ',{bold:true}),t('This part sets out what the lattice cannot do. It is given at the same length and the same standard of evidence as the positive results, for two reasons. A boundary established by computation is a result in the same sense that a theorem is, and several of the limits recorded here foreclose lines of attack that the earlier parts would otherwise invite. Every predictive application attempted in the course of this work is reported, including those that failed, with the diagnosis in each case.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:200}}));

B.push(h2('22.  The Metric Obstruction'));
B.push(h3('22.1  Λ is a subset of ℕ³'));
B.push(p([t('Every axis of the lattice is a count. The principal quantum number enumerates radial nodes, the azimuthal number enumerates angular nodes, and k enumerates electrons. Λ is therefore a subset of ℕ³, and every quantity constructed from its coordinates — distances, displacements, rank functionals, any index whatever — is a pure number.')]));
B.push(p([t('This is not a gap awaiting repair. It is constitutive of the construction, and it bounds what the lattice can be asked to do. Any application requiring a dimensional prediction — an energy, a rate, a length — must import its scale from outside, and whatever success follows belongs to the imported quantity rather than to Λ.')]));
B.push(img('F15_limits.png',640,206));
B.push(cap(13,'The limits of the lattice. (a) Each axis is a count, so Λ ⊆ ℕ³ and every derived quantity is dimensionless. (b) The metric-axis dilemma: a fourth coordinate independent of (n, ℓ, k) carries no lattice information, while one that is a function of them turns the admissible set into a functional graph and destroys closure, with 14,293 failures of each kind. (c) Every predictive application attempted in this work, with the established baseline marked where one exists.'));
B.push(h3('22.2  No metric coordinate can be adjoined'));
B.push(p([t('The obvious response to Section 22.1 is to add a fourth axis carrying metric information — an orbital energy, a screening parameter, a Slater–Condon integral. The proposal fails, and for a reason sharper than the dimensionlessness itself.')]));
B.push(p([t('Any metric quantity worth adding is a function of the existing coordinates; that is precisely what makes it informative about the lattice. But a coordinate determined by the others is not an independent axis of a product order. Adjoining it turns Λ from a product region into the graph of a function, and functional graphs are not sublattices of the ambient product. Direct test with a discretised energy proxy confirms the consequence: 14,293 join failures and 14,293 meet failures out of the pairs available.')]));
B.push(p([t('The choice is therefore stark. A metric axis independent of (n, ℓ, k) carries no information about the lattice and is decorative. A metric axis dependent on (n, ℓ, k) destroys the closure on which every result of Part II rests. This obstruction is distinct from that of Section 22.1 and arguably sharper: it bites even when a metric quantity is supplied free of charge.')]));
B.push(p([t('What remains available is labelling rather than coordinatisation. Metric data may be attached to lattice cells, with Λ serving as an index structure and the metric content coming from outside. Section 23 reports what happens when that is done.')]));

B.push(h2('23.  Applications Attempted'));
B.push(p([t('Five predictive applications were attempted. All five failed or were outperformed. They are reported together, with diagnoses, because the pattern across them is more informative than any one.')]));
B.push(h3('23.1  Hydride formation enthalpy'));
B.push(p([t('The enthalpy of hydride formation is measured, tabulated, dimensional, independent of this framework, and governs whether a metal absorbs hydrogen at all. Values for thirty-nine transition metals, lanthanides and actinides give')]));
B.push(eq('ΔH_f  =  +136.2  −  89.5·ℓ  +  6.4·k        (kJ · mol⁻¹ H)','15'));
B.push(p([t('with both coefficients significant — ℓ at p = 3.1 × 10⁻⁸ and k at p = 8.1 × 10⁻⁴ — an in-sample R² of 0.592 and a leave-one-out cross-validated R² of 0.514. Adding n, the configuration-space distance, or higher terms does not improve the cross-validated fit. Atomic number alone gives a negative cross-validated R², carrying no predictive information on this data.')]));
B.push(img('F13_hydride.png',640,243));
B.push(cap(14,'The lattice coordinates against hydride formation enthalpy for 39 metals. (a) Measured versus predicted for Equation (15); the dashed line is parity. (b) Variance explained by candidate models, in-sample and cross-validated. (c) Permutation null for the contribution of k: the observed gain of 0.15 in R² lies outside the distribution from 10,000 shuffles, at p = 0.0004.'));
B.push(p([t('Taken alone this looks encouraging. It should not be taken alone. Two schemes have addressed this problem for decades, and benchmarking against them under identical cross-validation is unflattering.')]));
B.push(img('F14_benchmark.png',640,223));
B.push(cap(15,'The lattice weighed against prior art. (a) Cross-validated R² for each scheme, with the number of fitted predictors marked. Pettifor\'s single-variable scale outperforms the two-variable lattice model by a wide margin, and Miedema\'s full form outperforms both. (b) Absolute correlation of the lattice coordinates with the established descriptors: the block index ℓ is strongly correlated with all three, whereas the occupancy k is uncorrelated with all three. (c) Cross-validated R² of each established scheme before and after adding k, with permutation significance.'));
B.push(tcap(2,'Prediction of hydride formation enthalpy across 39 metals, all schemes fitted and cross-validated identically. Paired Wilcoxon tests compare squared leave-one-out errors against the lattice model.'));
B.push(table(
  ['Scheme','Predictors','Fit R²','CV R²','CV RMSE','vs. lattice'],
  [['Miedema, full form','4','0.934','0.895','18.1','p < 0.0001'],
   ['Pettifor, MN + MN²','2','0.854','0.828','23.2','p < 0.0001'],
   ['Miedema, φ* + n_ws','2','0.842','0.808','24.6','p = 0.0001'],
   ['Pettifor, MN alone','1','0.808','0.790','25.7','p = 0.0003'],
   ['Lattice, ℓ + n + k','3','0.608','0.504','39.4','—'],
   ['Lattice, ℓ + k','2','0.592','0.514','39.1','—'],
   ['Atomic number Z','1','0.028','−0.076','58.1','p < 0.0001']],
  [2300,1300,1000,1000,1100,1500],['Lattice, ℓ + k']));
B.push(p([t('The result runs against the framework and is stated plainly. Pettifor\'s scale, using a single variable, achieves a cross-validated R² of 0.790 against the lattice\'s 0.514 with two; Miedema\'s full form reaches 0.895. Paired Wilcoxon tests reject equality against every established scheme, the weakest at p = 0.0003. Nor does combination rescue the position: adding ℓ and k to the full Miedema model changes its cross-validated R² from 0.895 to 0.888, a decrease.')],
  {spacing:{before:160,after:120}}));
B.push(p([t('One result survives, and it is worth isolating precisely because the rest did not. The two lattice coordinates behave quite differently against the established descriptors. The block index ℓ correlates strongly with all of them, at absolute values between 0.68 and 0.77, which is expected since ℓ is the block label and every chemical scale encodes block membership. The occupancy k is uncorrelated with all three — r = −0.05 against the Mendeleev number, +0.14 against Miedema\'s φ*, −0.13 against n_ws — none approaching significance. Adding k to Pettifor\'s Mendeleev number raises cross-validated R² from 0.790 to 0.844, a gain significant at p = 0.001 by permutation, with smaller but significant gains for the quadratic Pettifor form and the two-parameter Miedema form. Against the full four-parameter Miedema model the gain vanishes. The defensible claim is narrow: valence subshell occupancy carries information about hydride stability that Pettifor\'s chemical scale does not encode, and appending it improves that scale measurably. This is a contribution to an existing method, not an alternative to it.')]));
B.push(p([t('A related result belongs here. The classification by ΔQ direction from Section 6, derived purely from the angular-momentum constraint with no reference to any measurement, separates the same data: Class C elements average −61.9 kJ · mol⁻¹ H against −15.7 for Class B, significant at p = 0.010 by Welch\'s test. It is the only instance in this work of a purely geometric construction tracking a measured quantity without assistance.')]));
B.push(h3('23.2  Anomalous ground-state configurations'));
B.push(p([t('Twenty elements have documented ground states departing from strict aufbau filling. If the lattice carried information about them, anomalous elements should be enriched at structurally distinguished positions. They are not. Anomalous elements sit at half-filled subshells in 10 per cent of cases against a baseline of 16 per cent, at filled subshells in 5 per cent against 16 per cent, and on the kainosymmetric boundary in 20 per cent against 27 per cent. Every enrichment runs the wrong way.')]));
B.push(p([t('The diagnosis is instructive. Anomalies arise from competition between promotion energy and exchange stabilisation, a metric effect, and Section 22 establishes that the lattice cannot see metric effects. The point is sharpened by the false positives of any lattice-based rule: tungsten at (5, 2, 4) is regular while molybdenum at (4, 2, 4) is anomalous, and the two differ only in n. The distinction between them is relativistic contraction of the 6s orbital, which scales with Z² and is invisible to a coordinate that records only which shell is occupied.')]));
B.push(h3('23.3  Exchange-energy labelling'));
B.push(p([t('Section 22.2 leaves open the labelling of lattice cells with metric data. This was tried for the anomaly problem. Hund\'s first rule gives the number of parallel-spin pairs in a subshell directly from (ℓ, k), so the exchange stabilisation available on promoting an electron is computable from lattice coordinates alone. Labelling every cell with its exchange-pair count and testing whether the resulting gain separates anomalous from normal elements gives no separation: areas under the receiver-operating curve cluster around 0.5 across every variant tried, the best reaching 0.589 at p = 0.23. The textbook half-filled and one-below-full heuristic achieves precision 0.42 and recall 0.26. The exchange calculation is correct; what it lacks is the promotion cost, which is not a function of lattice position.')]));
B.push(h3('23.4  Ionisation energy and electronegativity'));
B.push(p([t('First ionisation energy is predicted at cross-validated R² = 0.42, but only by importing the hydrogenic 1/n² dependence, which is external physics rather than lattice structure; the lattice coordinates alone reach 0.19. Electronegativity is essentially unpredicted at 0.12. Both are recorded as attempted and unsuccessful.')]));
B.push(h3('23.5  What the pattern shows'));
B.push(p([t('Across five applications the pattern is consistent. Structural questions about Λ — its dimension, closure, invariants, ideals, correspondences — yield results, and several are exact. Metric questions do not, and the failures are diagnosable in each case to the same cause. The lattice organises; it does not predict. That is a limitation, and it is also a reasonably precise characterisation of what kind of object Λ is.')]));

B.push(h2('24.  Conclusion'));
B.push(p([t('The construction of Part I is a change of coordinates and nothing more: the Pauli principle and the angular-momentum condition, written as constraints on three integer axes. What it produces is a partial order, and the substance of this paper is an account of that order.')]));
B.push(p([t('Part II contains the results of which this work can be most confident, because they are structural facts checkable by direct computation rather than correlations with data. The order dimension of Λ is exactly three, so the third coordinate is necessary and not a convenience. The admissible cells form a bounded distributive lattice whose unique minimum is hydrogen, and closure follows from a standard sublattice argument that also shows why the hydrogenic labelling cannot replace the occupancy coordinate. Shell capacities emerge as slice volumes for n ≤ 5; the lattice is Sperner without being rank-symmetric; its rank generating function vanishes at q = −1, an exact parity balance traceable to spin. The Madelung rule is a linear functional whose level sets pair to give the period lengths, and projection along it reproduces Janet\'s left-step table exactly, while fibration along the n-axis reproduces the chemical groups and places helium among the alkaline earths by a second and independent route. The kainosymmetric subshells occupy precisely the boundary diagonal. And the orientation of the lattice is rigid: exactly one of 120 subshell orderings survives, and the Madelung functional is essentially unique among small-integer shears.')]));
B.push(p([t('Part III locates the periodic system within that structure. The 118 occupied cells form an order ideal, a property inherited from the monotone character of aufbau filling and robust to every excited-state extension that could be constructed. The ideal is all-or-nothing by column, making the realised system one of 120 admissible configurations rather than one of a far larger family, and leaving three maximal cells with five covering addresses for any further element.')]));
B.push(p([t('Part IV establishes the relation that most sharply characterises the lattice. Atomic number and Madelung order are linear extensions of Λ, reversing none of its comparable pairs; Pettifor\'s scale is not order-compatible in either orientation. Λ is a coarsening of atomic number that never contradicts it, and is orthogonal to structural sorting. The obstruction to a linear relation between Λ and the Pettifor scale is a theorem, not a difficulty of fitting.')]));
B.push(p([t('Part V sets the limits. Λ ⊆ ℕ³, so every derived quantity is dimensionless, and no metric coordinate can be adjoined without destroying the closure that the positive results depend on. Five predictive applications were attempted and none succeeded competitively. One narrow empirical result survives benchmarking: the occupancy coordinate carries information about hydride stability that established chemical scales do not encode, and improves Pettifor\'s scale measurably.')]));
B.push(p([t('Read together, the parts describe a combinatorial object with genuine order-theoretic structure, a definite and in places exact relationship to existing periodic formalisms, modest empirical purchase through one of its coordinates, and no reach into metric or nuclear physics. The field equation from which this framework originally took its name is, on the evidence assembled here, its least substantial component. The lattice is the contribution, and the paper is named for it.')]));

