h2('13.  The Occupied Cells Form an Order Ideal'));
B.push(p([t('An order ideal, or down-set, of a poset is a subset closed downward: if x belongs to it, so does every element below x. Testing this for the occupied cells of Λ — for every occupied x and every admissible y with y ≤ x componentwise, is y also occupied? — returns no violations across all 118 cells. The downward closure of the occupied set is exactly the occupied set.')]));
B.push(p([t('In chemical terms the statement is this. If an element exists whose ground-state valence configuration is (n, ℓ, k), then every configuration with no larger shell, no larger subshell and no larger occupancy is likewise realised by some element. There are no gaps beneath any occupied cell. The test is not vacuous: the number of admissible cells lying below an occupied cell ranges from one to ninety-two across the 118, and every one of those cells is occupied, as Figure 9(b) records.')]));
B.push(img('F10_ideal.png',640,211));
